d taking metformin twice a day.\n\nShe has been sleeping well and is able to sleep a full 8 hours. She denies thoughts of self-harm or suicidal ideations.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Neurological/Psychological: Grip strength equal bilaterally. Neurologically intact.\n• Neck: Supple without thyromegaly or lymphadenopathy. No carotid bruits appreciable.\nLungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate. Grade 2/6 systolic ejection fraction murmur. No gallops or rubs. No extra heart sounds. Good pulses.\n\nRESULTS\n\nHemoglobin A1c is elevated.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nBeverly Ortiz is a 24-year-old female patient with a history of type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure. She is here today for her yearly exam.\n\nDiabetes mellitus type 2.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her blood glucose levels have been uncontrolled. She is still taking metformin twice a day, but does admit to dietary indiscretion with an increase in her sweets intake.. Her most recent hemoglobin A1c was elevated.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue metformin twice a day.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged her to check her fasting blood glucose levels each morning and call them in about 2 weeks. She will follow up in about 2 months, at which time we will discuss further treatment recommendations.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: Appears to be relatively stable.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 10 mg once daily.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Hi Beverly, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient]: Hi, I\'m doing well, thank you.\n\n[doctor]: Great to hear. So, you\'re here for your annual exam. How have you been feeling overall?\n\n[patient]: I\'ve been feeling good, but I have been having some issues with my diabetes lately.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, okay. Can you tell me more about what\'s been going on with your diabetes?\n\n[patient]: Sure. I\'ve noticed my blood sugars have been all over the place recently. I think it\'s because of my new job at the bakery. I\'ve been eating more sweets because of it.\n\n[doctor]: I see. How long have you been working at the bakery?\n\n[patient]: I started this summer.\n\n[doctor]: And how have you been managing your diabetes otherwise? Are you taking your medication regularly?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I take my metformin twice a day and I haven\'t missed any doses.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s good to hear. How about your blood pressure? Have you noticed any changes there?\n\n[patient]: My blood pressure has been good, thankfully.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s good to hear. Are you still taking your lisinopril?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I take it once a day every day.\n\n[doctor]: Excellent. How have you been sleeping? Are you getting enough rest?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I sleep about 8 hours a night and I feel rested.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s great. Have you had any thoughts of self-harm or suicidal ideations recently?\n\n[patient]: No, none at all.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s good to hear. Let\'s move on to the physical exam. I\'ll start with your neurological and psychological status. Can you squeeze my hands as hard as you can?\n\n[patient]: Sure. (squeezes hands)\n\n[doctor]: Great, your grip strength is equal bilaterally. Now, let me check your neck. (palpates neck) Your neck is supple without any swelling or lumps. No carotid bruits are appreciable. Now, I\'ll listen to your lungs. Take a deep breath for me. (listens to lungs) Your lungs are clear without any wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n\n[patient]: That\'s good to know.\n\n[doctor]: Now, I\'ll check your heart. (listens to heart) Your heart rate is regular, but I do hear a grade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur. There are no gallops or rubs, and your pulses are good.\n\n[patient]: Okay.\n\n[doctor]: I see that your recent hemoglobin A1c was elevated. This indicates that your diabetes is not well controlled at the moment.\n\n[patient]: Oh, I see. What should I do about that?\n\n[doctor]: We\'ll continue with your metformin twice a day. I also encourage you to check your fasting blood glucose levels each morning and call them in about 2 weeks. We will discuss further treatment recommendations during your follow-up in about 2 months.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I can do that.\n\n[doctor]: Your blood pressure seems to be relatively stable, so continue taking your lisinopril 10 mg once daily.\n\n[patient]: Got it.\n\n[doctor]: Do you have any questions or concerns?\n\n[patient]: No, I think that covers everything.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, Beverly. It was good to see you today. We\'ll see you in about 2 months for your follow-up.\n\n[patient]: Thank you, doctor.\n\n[doctor]: You\'re welcome. Take care! }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Good morning, Beverly. How are you feeling today?\nPatient: Good morning, I\'m feeling okay.\nDoctor: That\'s great. So, I see that you\'re here for your annual exam today. Can you tell me a little bit about your history?\nPatient: Yes, I have type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure.\nDoctor: Okay, and how have you been feeling lately?\nPatient: I\'ve been doing well, but my diabetes is uncontrolled.\nDoctor: I see. Can you tell me more about that?\nPatient: My blood sugars have been fluctuating lately, and I think it\'s because of my change in diet. I started working at a bakery this summer, and I\'ve been eating more sweets.\nDoctor: I understand. Have you been taking your medication as prescribed?\nPatient: Yes, I have. I haven\'t missed any doses.\nDoctor: That\'s good to hear. And how has your blood pressure been?\nPatient: It\'s been good, even though my blood sugar levels have been up and down.\nDoctor: Okay, that\'s good to know. And how have you been sleeping?\nPatient: I\'ve been sleeping well, I can sleep a full 8 hours.\nDoctor: That\'s great. Have you had any thoughts of self-harm or suicidal ideations?\nPatient: No, I haven\'t.\nDoctor: Alright, that\'s good to hear. Now, let\'s move on to your physical examination. During the exam, I found that your grip strength is equal bilaterally, and your neurological system is intact. Your neck is supple without any swelling or lumps, and your lungs are clear to auscultation. Your heart rate is regular, and there\'s a mild systolic ejection fraction murmur, but no extra heart sounds. Your pulses are good.\nPatient: Hmm, okay.\nDoctor: I also checked your hemoglobin A1c, and it\'s elevated. That means your diabetes is not under control.\nPatient: Okay.\nDoctor: I recommend that you continue taking your medication, metformin, twice a day, and try to improve your diet by reducing your intake of sweets. I also encourage you to check your fasting blood glucose levels each morning and call me in about 2 weeks. We\'ll discuss further treatment options in 2 months.\nPatient: Okay, I understand.\nDoctor: Great. And I see that you also have high blood pressure. That\'s stable for now, so we\'ll continue with your current treatment, lisinopril 10 mg once daily.\nPatient: Okay.\nDoctor: Is there anything else you would like to ask or discuss?\nPatient: No, I think that\'s all.\nDoctor: Alright, if you have any questions or concerns in the future, don\'t hesitate to reach out to me. Have a great day.\nPatient: Thank you, doctor.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\n[doctor] good afternoon , beverly . good to see you .\n[patient] nice to see you too .\n[doctor] so , i know my nurse told you a little bit about dax . um , now , if you do n\'t mind , i\'d like to tell dax a little bit about you .\n[patient] go for it .\n[doctor] great . um , beverly\'s a 24 year old female patient , and i know you have a history of type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure . correct ?\n[patient] yep .\n[doctor] okay . so , you\'re here today for your yearly exam . have n\'t seen you in a year . how\'re you doing ?\n[patient] i\'ve been doing well . i began working in a bakery this summer which 