n going really well with my medication and therapy. I\'ve been trying to get outside more and it\'s been helping with my depression. I\'m sleeping and eating well and I don\'t have any concerns about hurting myself or others.\n\nDoctor: That\'s great to hear. And how has your carpal tunnel been?\n\nPatient: It\'s been doing really well. I\'ve been getting full range of motion and it\'s felt a lot better.\n\nDoctor: That\'s good to hear. Let\'s take a look at your physical examination. Your neurological and psychological exam shows appropriate mood and affect. Your grips are equal bilaterally. Your neck is supple without thyromegaly or cervical lymphadenopathy. Your lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally and there are no wheezes, rales, or rhonchi. Your cardiovascular exam shows a regular rate and rhythm with no murmurs, gallops, or rubs and no extra heart sounds. Your musculoskeletal exam shows no lower extremity edema, but there is pain with patellar inhibition and pain with range of motion of the right knee.\n\nDoctor: We also did an x-ray of your right knee and it was unremarkable.\n\nDoctor: Based on your history and examination, we have diagnosed you with Chondromalacia of the right patella. This is a condition where the cartilage under the patella is soft and worn away. It\'s not uncommon in active individuals like yourself. We have prescribed ibuprofen 600 mg 4 times a day with food to help with the pain. We also want you to do some straight leg raising exercises to strengthen your quadriceps. It\'s important to participate in physical activity that is less straining on your joints, such as swimming freestyle laps, or to stop activity altogether. If your pain does not improve in a couple of weeks, we will need to follow up.\n\nDoctor: Your chronic back pain is stable and well-controlled at this time, so we don\'t need to make any changes to your current regimen. And your depression is also doing well with your current medications, so we will continue with your current regimen.\n\nDoctor: And your carpal tunnel syndrome is relatively stable status post carpal tunnel release, so we won\'t be making any changes to your current regimen at this time.\n\nDoctor: Do you have any questions or concerns about the treatment plan?\n\nPatient: No, I understand and agree with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nDoctor: Great. If you have any questions or concerns in the future, don\'t hesitate to reach out to us.\n\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\n[doctor] good morning rebecca , nice to see you .\n[patient] nice to see you too .\n[doctor] so , my nurse told you a little bit about dax , now i\'m wondering if i can tell dax a little bit about you .\n[patient] go for it .\n[doctor] okay , so rebecca is a 27-year-old female patient . um , you have a history of carpal tunnel , um , uh , release done couple of years ago , you have some chronic back pain , some depression . why am i seeing you today ?\n[patient] so last week , um , and over the course of the month , i have taken up swimming more regularly .\n[doctor] okay .\n[patient] i\'ve been trying to get up and get active . and i\'ve- ... all of a sudden i\'ve been feeling some really bad , um , knee pain-\n[doctor] okay .\n[patient] . in my right knee .\n[doctor] just in your right knee ?\n[patient] yeah .\n[doctor] okay . just when swimming ? are you swimming freestyle with your legs kicking straight or more like breaststroke where your legs kick out ?\n[patient] yeah , it\'s only while swimming. i use the breaststroke so i guess where the legs are kicking out like a frog ... it kinda starts hurting a little bit after i swim a couple laps , which a month ago when i started i was able to do ten .\n[doctor] okay .\n[patient] but after a couple of laps it starts to hurt .\n[doctor] and no injury ? like you did n\'t fall or twist it or anything like that that you remember ?\n[patient] no , not that i can remember .\n[doctor] okay . so just swimming a lot and you feelin