-d '{'model': 'gpt-4o', 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nJoint pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Janet Sullivan is a 33-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of joint pain.\n\nShe localizes the joint pain to her bilateral knees. The patient reports she has been doing stair workouts, which she thought was building muscle on her knees. She denies any pins and needles in her feet or swelling in her legs. The patient notes that she would look out for those symptoms because she has diabetes. She denies any fever or chills. She denies any nausea or vomiting. The patient denies any chest pain or shortness of breath.\n\nShe notes she had a right shoulder rotator cuff repair about 2 years ago, but she has not had any problems since that point.\n\nShe reports that her diabetes are well-controlled.\n\nThe patient does have high blood pressure but she monitors it and takes Norvasc 5 mg a day. She notes her blood pressure has not been elevated since her joint pain started.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Negative for fever, chills\n• Cardiovascular: Negative for chest pain or lower leg swelling.\n• Respiratory: Negative for shortness of breath.\n• Gastrointestinal: Negative for nausea, vomiting.\n• Musculoskeletal: Positive for Arthralgias\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\nNeck\n• General Examination: Neck is supple without thyromegaly.\n\nRespiratory\n• Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n\nCardiovascular\n• Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n• Left knee: Slight erythema and edema. Small effusion present. Decreased flexion and extension. Strength in lower extremities is good.\n\nRESULTS\n\nViews: Four views left knee.\nIndication: Knee pain.\nInterpretation: There are no fractures, dislocations, or other abnormalities.\n\nPLAN\n\nMs. Janet Sullivan is a 33-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of joint pain.\n\nLeft knee strain.\n• Medical Treatment: We will order an autoimmune panel for further workup. I am going to refer her to physical therapy and prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once daily to help with pain.\n• Specialist Referrals: Physical therapy.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her blood pressure is elevated in office today, but has been well-controlled on Norvasc 5 mg daily based on home monitoring.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue current regimen and home monitoring. She will send me a report in the next couple of weeks. We will also order a lipid profile.\n\nDiabetes type II.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her hemoglobin a1C is slightly elevated, but her blood glucose levels have been well-controlled.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue metformin 500 mg twice a day.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] Good morning, Ms. Sullivan. How are you doing today?\n\n[patient] Good morning, doctor. I\'ve been better. I\'m here because of this joint pain in my knees.\n\n[doctor] I see. Can you tell me more about the joint pain you\'re experiencing?\n\n[patient] Sure. It\'s in both of my knees, and it\'s been bothering me for a while now. I thought it was getting better, but it\'s still there.\n\n[doctor] When did you first start noticing the pain?\n\n[patient] It started a few weeks ago. I thought it was just from doing stair workouts, but it hasn\'t gone away.\n\n[doctor] I see. How would you describe the pain? Is it sharp, dull, throbbing?\n\n[patient] It\'s more of a dull ache, but it can get pretty intense sometimes, especially when I move.\n\n[doctor] Does the pain radiate anywhere else, or is it localized to your knees?\n\n[patient] It\'s just in my knees.\n\n[doctor] Do you have any other symptoms, like pins and needles in your feet or swelling in your legs?\n\n[patient] No, I don\'t have any of that. I do have diabetes, so I keep an eye out for those symptoms.\n\n[doctor] That\'s good to know. Have you had any fever or chills?\n\n[patient] No, none of that either.\n\n[doctor] How about nausea or vomiting?\n\n[patient] No, I haven\'t had any nausea or vomiting.\n\n[doctor] Any chest pain or shortness of breath?\n\n[patient] No, my chest feels fine, and I haven\'t had any trouble breathing.\n\n[doctor] Okay, that\'s good to hear. I see here that you had a right shoulder rotator cuff repair about 2 years ago. Have you had any issues since then?\n\n[patient] No, the shoulder has been fine since the surgery.\n\n[doctor] That\'s great. How are your diabetes management going?\n\n[patient] My diabetes are well-controlled.\n\n[doctor] And your blood pressure? I see you\'re on Norvasc 5 mg daily. Have you noticed any changes?\n\n[patient] My blood pressure has been okay. It hasn\'t been elevated since the joint pain started.\n\n[doctor] Alright. Let\'s go through a quick review of systems. Any fever or chills?\n\n[patient] No, none.\n\n[doctor] Any chest pain or lower leg swelling?\n\n[patient] No, none of that.\n\n[doctor] Shortness of breath?\n\n[patient] No, my breathing is fine.\n\n[doctor] Any nausea or vomiting?\n\n[patient] No, none.\n\n[doctor] Okay, and you mentioned joint pain. Any other joint pain or muscle aches?\n\n[patient] Just the knees.\n\n[doctor] Alright, let\'s move on to the physical exam. I\'ll start with your neck. Your neck is supple without any swelling or lumps. Now, let me listen to your lungs. Your lungs are clear bilaterally, no wheezes, rales, or rhonchi. Your heart sounds are regular with no murmurs, gallops, or rubs. Now, let\'s take a look at your knees. I see slight erythema and edema in your left knee. There\'s a small effusion, and you have decreased flexion and extension. Your strength in the lower extremities is good. \n\n[patient] Okay.\n\n[doctor] Based on the physical exam, it looks like you have some joint pain in your left knee. We\'ll need to get an X-ray to rule out any fractures or dislocations. \n\n[patient] Alright.\n\n[doctor] I\'ll also order an autoimmune panel to check for any underlying issues. In the meantime, I\'m going to refer you to physical therapy and prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once daily to help with the pain.\n\n[patient] Okay, that sounds good.\n\n[doctor] I also noticed that your blood pressure was elevated today. Have you been monitoring it at home?\n\n[patient] Yes, I have. It\'s usually well-controlled with the Norvasc.\n\n[doctor] Alright, continue with your current regimen and keep monitoring it. I\'ll also order a lipid profile to check your cholesterol levels.\n\n[patient] Got it.\n\n[doctor] Your hemoglobin A1C is slightly elevated, but your blood glucose levels have been well-controlled. Continue taking metformin 500 mg twice a day.\n\n[patient] Will do.\n\n[doctor] Do you have any questions or concerns about the treatment plan?\n\n[patient] No, everything sounds good. Thank you, doctor.\n\n[doctor] You\'re welcome. We\'ll get the X-ray done and follow up with the results. I\'ll see you in a couple of weeks to review everything.\n\n[patient] Thank you, doctor. See you then.\n\n[doctor] Take care, Ms. Sullivan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ \nDoctor: Good morning, Ms. Janet. How can I help you today?\n\nPatient: Good morning, doctor. I have been experiencing joint pain in my knees.\n\nDoctor: I see. Can you tell me more about your joint pain? Where exactly is the pain located?\n\nPatient: The pain is in both of my knees.\n\nDoctor: Hmm, I see. Have you been doing any strenuous activities lately that could have caused the pain?\n\nPatient: Yes, I have been doing stair workouts.\n\nDoctor: I see. And have you noticed any pins and needles in your feet or swelling in your legs?\n\nPatient: No, I haven\'t noticed any pins and needles or swelling in my legs.\n\nDoctor: That\'s good. Do you have any other symptoms like fever or chills?\n\nPatient: No, I don\'t have any fever or chills.\n\nDoctor: How about nausea or vomiting?\n\nPatient: No, I haven\'t had any nausea or vomiting.\n\nDoctor: And have you experienced any chest pain or shortness of breath?\n\nPatient: No, I haven\'t had any chest pain or shortness of breath.\n\nDoctor: I see. Have you had any medical conditions in the past that I should know about?\n\nPatient: Yes, I had a right shoulder rotator cuff repair about 2 years ago.\n\nDoctor: Okay, and how has your diabetes been controlled?\n\nPatient: My diabetes is well-controlled.\n\nDoctor: I see. And what about your blood pressure? Have you been monitoring it?\n\nPatient: Yes, I have high blood pressure, but I monitor it and take Norvasc 5 mg a day. My blood pressure has not been elevated since my joint pain started.\n\nDoctor: That\'s good. Let\'s move on to the physical examination. During the examination, I noticed that your left knee has slight erythema and edema, and there is a small effusion present. Can you tell me more about that?\n\nPatient: Yes, I have been experiencing pain in my left knee.\n\nDoctor: I see. Based on the examination and test results, I have diagnosed you with a left knee strain. I have ordered an autoimmune panel for further workup and referred you to physical therapy. I have also prescribed meloxicam 15 mg once daily to help with the pain.\n\nPatient: Okay, thank you, doctor.\n\nDoctor: You\'re welcome. I would like to see you in a couple of weeks to check on your progress. And, I would like to continue your current regimen for your hypertension and diabetes. Please keep me updated on your home monitoring.\n\nPatient: Okay, I will.\n\nDoctor: Great. Is there anything else you would like to ask or discuss?\n\nPatient: No, that\'s all. Thank you, doctor.\n\nDoctor: You\'re welcome. Take care.\n\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\n[doctor] hi janet , how are you ?\n[patient] good , how are you ?\n[doctor] i\'m good . uh , are you ready to get started ?\n[patient] yes .\n[doctor] okay . so , janet is a 33-year-old female , here for some , some joint paint . what , what joint\'s been bothering you , janet ?\n[patient] it\'s been mostly , um , my knees , actually . i\'ve been doing more stair type workouts , which i thought was , you know , building muscle , uh , on my knees and supposed to help .\n[doctor] mm-hmm .\n[patient] but in turn it , it really has n\'t , so my knees have been really bothering me .\n[doctor] okay . all right . and is it one knee versus the other ? or both equally ?\n[patient] it\'s both equally .\n[doctor] okay . and have you been having any other joint pain like , elbows or shoulder , or anything like that ?\n[patient] no , but i , i should mention that like , i had a rotator cuff repair about two years ago .\n[doctor] mm-hmm .\n[patient] um , but i have n\'t had any problems since that point .