rvical lymphadenopathy. I\'m going to check your lungs now. They\'re clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi. I\'m going to check your heart now. The rate and rhythm are regular and there are no murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No extra heart sounds. Your blood pressure is elevated.\nPatient: Okay.\nDoctor: I\'m going to order some laboratory studies to make sure everything is within normal limits. The results are back and everything is within normal limits. Based on your history and physical examination, I\'m going to recommend some medical treatment for your hypertension. I\'m going to increase your lisinopril to 10 mg once daily. I also want to educate you on monitoring your blood pressure at home and following up in 1 month. Is that okay with you?\nPatient: Yes, that\'s fine.\nDoctor: Great. And I see that your carpal tunnel release is doing well 2 years postoperatively and you\'re asymptomatic at this time. If your symptoms return, please let us know. Is that understood?\nPatient: Yes, it is.\nDoctor: Alright, thank you. I\'ll see you in a month. Take care.\nPatient: Thank you, doctor.\nFamily: Excuse me, doctor. I\'m Christine\'s daughter. I just wanted to thank you for taking care of her.\nDoctor: Of course, it was my pleasure. If you have any questions or concerns, don\'t hesitate to reach out. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\n[doctor] morning christine , nice to see you .\n[patient] nice to see you too .\n[doctor] so i know my nurse told you a little bit of- ... um , about dax and i\'m gon na tell dax a little bit about you , if that\'s okay ?\n[patient] go for it .\n[doctor] okay , great . um , uh , this is christine . she\'s a 63-year-old female patient . you\'re here for your routine , um , yearly exam . um , i know you have a history of high blood pressure and you\'ve had some depression in the past . and i also know you did have a carpal tunnel release done about two years ago . um , how have you been doing ?\n[patient] i\'ve be