 and evening, stating it is higher at night but will improve after sitting for a few minutes. She is averaged 135-145 or 150 over 95-110.\n\nShe experienced side effects from lisinopril, such as headaches, “swooshing” in her left ear, and lethargy. She took half a dose and did not experience the side effects.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nHypertension.\nHypercholesterolemia.\nAlcohol use disorder.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nLisinopril 20 mg tablet once daily.\nHydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg once daily.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional\nNo distress. No fever. No tobacco or other substance use.\n\nNeurological\nAlert.\n\nPsychological\nShe does not appear anxious, agitated or depressed.\n\nNeck\nJVP flat with a head at 90 degrees.\n\nRespiratory\nNo cough or shortness of breath. Chest clear to percussion and auscultation. Unlabored breathing.\n\nCardiovascular\nNo CVA tenderness. Cardiac rhythm regular. No murmur. No gallop.\n\nMusculoskeletal\nNo ankle edema.\n\nIntegumentary\nSkin is moist. Good trigger.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Alcohol use disorder.\n• Hypertension.\n\nMrs. Taylor presents for a follow-up for hypertension. History includes significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At last visit on April 16, BP was 130/90. After presenting at 150/100 she was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.\n\nPLAN\n\nAlcohol use disorder\nI will start her on naltrexone which we have previously discussed. She will take the injectable form and start on May 17. We reviewed side effects and risks. The potential benefit of alcohol cessation would be highly beneficial to the patient.\n\nHypertension\nShe will continue taking lisinopril 10 mg/day and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. Quite possibly her blood pressure will improve significantly if she is able to completely stop consuming alcohol. Target BP is 130-140/70-80. I will see her back week of June 14 which likely will be the time of her second naltrexone injection.\n\nRecommend upgrading influenza vaccination and Shingrix #2, she defers this.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nCall the clinic with any questions. Make follow up appointment week of June 14th.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Hi Amanda, good to see you today. How are you feeling?\n\n[patient]: Hi, Doctor. I\'m doing okay, I guess. My blood pressure has been a bit high.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, I see. Let\'s talk about that. When did you last check your blood pressure?\n\n[patient]: I\'ve been checking it every morning and evening. It\'s usually around 135-145 over 95-110, but it\'s higher at night. Sometimes it drops a bit after I sit for a few minutes.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, that\'s helpful to know. Have you noticed any patterns or triggers that make your blood pressure higher?\n\n[patient]: Not really, it just seems to be high most of the time.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. I see from your records that you were prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg and lisinopril 20 mg at your last visit. How have you been feeling with those medications?\n\n[patient]: I\'ve been taking them, but I had some side effects. I felt headaches, this weird "swooshing" in my left ear, and I was really lethargic. So, I took half a dose and didn\'t have those issues.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, I see. It\'s important to take your medications as prescribed. Have you noticed any other side effects?\n\n[patient]: No, just those.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. I also see you\'re still consuming alcohol 4-5 times a week. How does that affect your blood pressure?\n\n[patient]: Yeah, I know I should cut down. I think my blood pressure would be better if I stopped drinking.\n\n[doctor]: Absolutely, stopping alcohol would likely help significantly. Have you considered any methods to help you quit?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I\'m interested in the Naltrexone injections. I\'ve heard they can help.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s a good idea. We can start you on Naltrexone injections. You\'ll start on May 17th. We discussed the side effects and risks before, but let\'s go over them again to make sure you\'re comfortable with it.\n\n[patient]: Yes, please. I want to try it.\n\n[doctor]: Great. Naltrexone can help reduce cravings and improve your chances of quitting alcohol. It\'s important to take it as prescribed and let us know if you experience any side effects. \n\n[patient]: Okay, I understand.\n\n[doctor]: For your hypertension, you\'ll continue taking lisinopril 10 mg and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. Your blood pressure should improve if you stop drinking alcohol. Our target range is 130-140/70-80.\n\n[patient]: Got it.\n\n[doctor]: Let\'s also review your physical exam. You appear to be in no distress, with no fever or other signs of illness. Your neurological exam shows you\'re alert, and psychologically, you don\'t appear anxious, agitated, or depressed.\n\n[patient]: That\'s good to hear.\n\n[doctor]: Your neck exam shows a flat jugular venous pressure with your head at 90 degrees, and your respiratory exam is normal with clear lungs and unlabored breathing. Cardiovascular exam shows no CVA tenderness, a regular heart rhythm, and no murmurs or gallops. Musculoskeletal exam shows no ankle edema, and your skin is moist and healthy.\n\n[patient]: Okay.\n\n[doctor]: We\'re still awaiting your lipid panel results to monitor your hypercholesterolemia. Have you had any issues with your cholesterol medications?\n\n[patient]: No, I\'ve been taking them as prescribed.\n\n[doctor]: Excellent. We\'ll continue to monitor that as well. Now, I recommend upgrading your influenza vaccination to the Shingrix vaccine. You mentioned you deferred it last time?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I did. \n\n[doctor]: It\'s important to get that updated. We\'ll schedule that for you. Do you have any other questions or concerns?\n\n[patient]: No, I think that\'s everything for now.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, Amanda. We\'ll see you back in the week of June 14th for your follow-up and second Naltrexone injection. If you have any questions or concerns before then, don\'t hesitate to call the clinic.\n\n[patient]: Thank you, Doctor.\n\n[doctor]: You\'re welcome. Take care, Amanda.\n\n[patient]: You too. Bye. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Good morning, Amanda. How are you feeling today?\nAmanda: Good morning, doctor. I\'m feeling okay.\nDoctor: That\'s great to hear. So, you\'re here for a follow-up for your hypertension, correct?\nAmanda: Yes, that\'s right.\nDoctor: Can you tell me a little bit about your history?\nAmanda: Well, I have a history of alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia.\nDoctor: I see. And at your last visit on April 16, your blood pressure was 130/90, correct?\nAmanda: Yes, that\'s right.\nDoctor: After that, you were prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg and lisinopril 20 mg, is that correct?\nAmanda: Yes, that\'s right.\nDoctor: And how have you been feeling since then?\nAmanda: I\'ve been feeling okay, but I\'ve been experiencing some side effects from the lisinopril, like headaches, "swooshing" in my left ear, and lethargy.\nDoctor: I see. And have you been taking the medications as prescribed?\nAmanda: Yes, I have.\nDoctor: Good. And have you been checking your blood pressure regularly?\nAmanda: Yes, I have. I\'ve been checking it in the morning and evening, and it\'s been higher at night, but it improves after I sit for a few minutes. It\'s been around 135-145 over 95-110.\nDoctor: I see. And have you been drinking alcohol as much as you used to?\nAmanda: No, I\'ve been drinking 4-5 times a week.\nDoctor: I see. And you mentioned that you\'re interested in Naltrexone injections. Can you tell me more about that?\nAmanda: Yes, I\'d like to try it to help with my alcohol use disorder.\nDoctor: I understand. And you mentioned that your blood pressure would likely improve if you stopped alcohol consumption. Is that correct?\nAmanda: Yes, that\'s what I\'ve been told.\nDoctor: I see. Well, I t