ot attended physical therapy.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMusculoskeletal\nLower right extremity: Swollen right knee, slightly reduced range of motion. Skin warm to touch. Lower right leg pits with pressure.\n\nPROCEDURE\n\nFluid aspiration and Steroid injection.\nRisks, benefits, and alternatives were explained. The risks of steroid injection were explained, including but not limited to, hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hyperglycemia among others. Verbal consent was obtained. The right knee was accessed through the lateral approach with 3 mL of initially clear fluid then blood-tinged fluid. Then the right knee was prepped in a standard fashion. The right knee was injected with 80 mg triamcinolone and 1 mL lidocaine. The procedure was tolerated well with no complications. Post procedure instructions were given.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Rheumatoid arthritis management.\n• High risk medication use.\n• Right knee pain.\n• Lower extremity pitting edema.\n\nPLAN\n\nRheumatoid arthritis management.\nThe patient will continue Xeljanz XR 11 mg daily. He will also continue Celebrex 200 mg daily.\n\nHigh-risk medication use.\nThe patient is currently on the high-risk medication Xeljanz. We will check his CMP levels and monitor periodically every couple of months.\n\nRight knee pain.\nHe has right knee pain and swelling, with pain level 6/10. On exam today, the right knee continues to be hot, which is ongoing inflammation. We performed a fluid aspiration of the right knee today, see procedure note above. A steroid injection to the right knee was given today, see procedure note above. We will arrange for x-ray of the right knee and refer his to physical therapy.\n\nLower extremity pitting edema.\nThe patient notes whole right leg swelling since the last visit. He usually wears support hose, which helps to some degree. He has not seen his PCP. We discussed compression stockings, elevate feet, and a low salt diet. He will check with his PCP for further instructions.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Good morning, Mr. Nelson. How are you doing today?\n\n[patient]: Good morning, Doctor. I\'ve been better, to be honest. The pain in my right knee is still bothering me a lot.\n\n[doctor]: I\'m sorry to hear that. Can you tell me more about the pain you\'re experiencing?\n\n[patient]: Sure. The pain is about a 6 out of 10. It gets worse when I stand up after sitting for a while. I have to use a cane to get my bearings.\n\n[doctor]: I see. And you mentioned at your last visit that the steroid injection only provided relief for a few weeks. Is that correct?\n\n[patient]: Yes, that\'s right. It helped for a little while, but then the pain came back.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, and you\'ve been experiencing swelling in your right leg since your last visit?\n\n[patient]: Yes, the whole right leg is swollen. I usually wear support hose, which helps a bit, but not completely.\n\n[doctor]: Have you been able to attend any physical therapy sessions?\n\n[patient]: No, I haven\'t attended any physical therapy yet.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, let\'s take a look at your knee. I\'ll start with a physical exam. Please let me know if you feel any discomfort.\n\n[patient]: Okay.\n\n[doctor]: I see that your right knee is swollen and has a slightly reduced range of motion. The skin is warm to the touch. Let me press on your lower leg. Does this hurt?\n\n[patient]: Yes, it does.\n\n[doctor]: Thank you for letting me know. Based on your symptoms and the physical exam, we\'ll need to do a fluid aspiration and another steroid injection to help with the inflammation and pain. We\'ll also get an x-ray of your knee to check for any joint space narrowing or osteophytes.\n\n[patient]: Alright, that sounds good.\n\n[doctor]: Before we proceed, let\'s discuss the risks of the steroid injection. These include hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hyperglycemia, among others. Are you okay with moving forward with this?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I understand the risks and I\'m willing to proceed.\n\n[doctor]: Great. We\'ll access your knee through the lateral approach. I\'ll inject 3 mL of initially clear fluid, and then blood-tinged fluid to ensure we get the right amount of fluid out. After that, we\'ll prepare the area and inject 80 mg of triamcinolone and 1 mL of lidocaine. Is that clear?\n\n[patient]: Yes, that\'s clear.\n\n[doctor]: Excellent. Let\'s get started. I\'ll be gentle, but if you feel any discomfort, please let me know immediately.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I will.\n\n[doctor]: (After the procedure) The procedure went well. There were no complications. You tolerated it well. Here are the post-procedure instructions. Make sure to keep the area clean and avoid any heavy lifting.\n\n[patient]: Thank you, Doctor. I\'ll follow the instructions.\n\n[doctor]: You\'re welcome. Now, regarding your rheumatoid arthritis, you\'ll continue taking Xeljanz XR 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 mg daily. We\'ll also check your CMP levels periodically every couple of months since you\'re on a high-risk medication.\n\n[patient]: Alright, I understand.\n\n[doctor]: For the right knee pain and swelling, we\'ll monitor how you respond to the steroid injection. If there\'s no improvement, we may need to consider other treatment options. I\'ll arrange for an x-ray of your knee and refer you to physical therapy.\n\n[patient]: Okay, that sounds good.\n\n[doctor]: Regarding the lower extremity pitting edema, I recommend continuing to wear support hose, elevating your feet when possible, and following a low-salt diet. I\'ll also refer you to your primary care physician for further instructions.\n\n[patient]: I\'ll do that. Thank you.\n\n[doctor]: You\'re welcome. Do you have any other questions or concerns?\n\n[patient]: No, I think that\'s everything for now.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, Mr. Nelson. We\'ll see you again soon. Take care and follow the instructions carefully.\n\n[patient]: Thank you, Doctor. I\'ll do that.\n\n[doctor]: You\'re welcome. Have a good day.\n\n[patient]: You too. Goodbye.\n\n[doctor]: Goodbye. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Good morning, Mr. Nelson. How are you feeling today?\nPatient: I\'m not feeling too good, doctor. My right knee is still causing me a lot of pain and swelling.\nDoctor: I see. You were last seen by me on March 4th, 2021, and you received a steroid injection to your right knee. How has that been working for you?\nPatient: It only helped for a few weeks. The pain level is still 6/10.\nDoctor: I understand. Have you noticed any swelling in your whole right leg since your last visit?\nPatient: Yes, I have.\nDoctor: And have you been wearing support hose to help with the swelling?\nPatient: Yes, it helps to some degree.\nDoctor: Have you attended physical therapy yet?\nPatient: No, I haven\'t.\nDoctor: Okay. We performed a fluid aspiration and steroid injection on your right knee today. We accessed the knee through the lateral approach and removed 3 mL of initially clear fluid then blood-tinged fluid. We then prepped the knee and injected it with 80 mg triamcinolone and 1 mL lidocaine. The procedure was tolerated well with no complications. Post-procedure instructions will be given.\nPatient: Okay.\nDoctor: We will continue your rheumatoid arthritis management with Xeljanz XR 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 mg daily. We will also monitor your CMP levels periodically every couple of months due to the high-risk medication use.\nPatient: Alright.\nDoctor: We will arrange for an x-ray of your right knee and refer you to physical therapy. In the meantime, I recommend wearing compression stockings, elevating your feet, and following a low salt diet to help with the swelling.\nPatient: Okay, I will check with my PCP for further instructions.\nDoctor: Great. Please come back for a follow-up visit in a couple of months so we can monitor your progress. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\n[doctor] tyler nelson , date of bi