-d '{'model': 'gpt-4o', 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up for perioral dermatitis.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJacqueline Miller is a 40-year-old female who is being seen today for a follow-up skin exam for perioral dermatitis. She also has a history of rosacea, including ocular rosacea. Today, she states that her perioral dermatitis is well-controlled, although it has not fully resolved. The patient was applying doxycycline twice a day with improvement, but her pharmacist advised her to reduce to once daily since it had started to improve; therefore, for approximately the past week, she has been using the doxycycline only once per day. She has not seen continued improvement but it has not worsened. She is using the sulfacetamide wash twice daily, denies drying of the skin, and reports that her skin is much improved moisturization. She also applies MetroCream immediately after using the sulfacetamide wash. She denies using other lotions and products, Cetaphil lotion or soap, or Neutrogena wipes. The patient did not begin Elidel because it is not covered by her insurance, and she was concerned about possible side effects from the medication.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nPerioral dermatitis.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nDoxycycline cream. Apply twice daily.\nMetroCream. \nSulfacetamide wash. Apply topically twice daily.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nIntegumentary\nSkin inspection of the face was performed today. On bilateral medial cheeks, there are a few, very faint, erythematous papules with mild redness around and underneath the nostrils. Fitzpatrick skin type is 4 to 5.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Perioral dermatitis\n• Rosacea, including ocular rosacea\n\nJacqueline Miller is a 40-year-old female who presented today for follow-up for her perioral dermatitis, which has improved but not resolved. She decreased her doxycycline per her pharmacist’s recommendation, which possibly stagnated the resolution of symptoms.\n\nPLAN\n\nPerioral dermatitis.\nI counseled the patient about the risks and benefits of doxycycline and recommended she resume twice daily application until she reaches full resolution, and at that point she should reduce application to once a day for an additional week. The patient will not be using Elidel as I reviewed the cost of the medication and without insurance, it was too expensive. I provided a prescription for Tacrolimus and counseled her on possible side effects of using the medication. She will continue with Sulfacetamide face wash once a day. Also, use Bruder mask as needed.\n\nRosacea, including ocular rosacea.\nContinue using MetroCream twice daily. I recommended to gently wash her eyelids once daily with a gentle cleanser and try a gentle cream such as Vanicream.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nCall the clinic if symptoms worsen. The patient will have a follow-up appointment in 4 weeks.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Hi Jacqueline, how are you today?\n\n[patient]: Hi, I\'m doing okay, thank you. I\'m here for a follow-up on my perioral dermatitis.\n\n[doctor]: Great, let\'s talk about how you\'ve been doing since your last visit. How has your perioral dermatitis been?\n\n[patient]: It\'s been a bit better, but it hasn\'t fully resolved. It\'s still there, but it hasn\'t gotten worse.\n\n[doctor]: I see. You mentioned that you were using doxycycline twice a day. How has that been going?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I was applying it twice a day, but my pharmacist advised me to reduce it to once daily since it had started to improve. So, for about a week now, I\'ve been using it only once a day.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, and have you noticed any changes in your symptoms since you reduced the dosage?\n\n[patient]: Not really. It hasn\'t gotten worse, but it hasn\'t improved much either.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, alright. And you\'re still using the sulfacetamide wash twice daily?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I am. My skin doesn\'t feel dry, and it\'s much better moisturized.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s good to hear. Are you applying MetroCream immediately after using the sulfacetamide wash?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I am. I haven\'t used any other lotions or products like Cetaphil lotion, soap, or Neutrogena wipes.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s good. You mentioned that you were concerned about using Elidel because of the cost and possible side effects. Is that still the case?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I was worried about the cost and the side effects. I decided not to use it.\n\n[doctor]: Understandable. Let\'s take a look at your face. I\'ll perform a skin inspection now. \n\n[patient]: Okay.\n\n[doctor]: On inspection, I see a few very faint erythematous papules with mild redness on your bilateral medial cheeks and around and underneath your nostrils. Your skin type is 4 to 5. \n\n[patient]: What does that mean?\n\n[doctor]: It means you have a moderate skin type. It\'s prone to dryness and irritation but also has some oiliness. Now, regarding your perioral dermatitis, it seems like reducing the doxycycline might have stalled your progress. I recommend you resume the twice daily application until you see full resolution, and then you can go back to once a day for an additional week.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I can do that.\n\n[doctor]: I also want to discuss the rosacea. You have ocular rosacea as well, correct?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I do.\n\n[doctor]: Continue using MetroCream twice daily. I also recommend gently washing your eyelids once daily with a gentle cleanser and using a gentle cream like Vanicream.\n\n[patient]: Alright, I can do that.\n\n[doctor]: You should continue with the Sulfacetamide face wash once a day and use a Bruder mask as needed. \n\n[patient]: Got it.\n\n[doctor]: If your symptoms worsen, please call the clinic. We\'ll have a follow-up appointment in 4 weeks to reassess.\n\n[patient]: Okay, thank you, doctor.\n\n[doctor]: You\'re welcome. Take care, Jacqueline.\n\n[patient]: Thank you. Bye.\n\n[doctor]: Bye. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ \nDoctor: Good morning, Jacqueline. How are you today?\n\nPatient: I\'m good, thank you.\n\nDoctor: That\'s great. I understand you\'re here for a follow-up for your perioral dermatitis. Can you tell me a little bit about your history?\n\nPatient: Yes, I have a history of rosacea, including ocular rosacea, and I was seen for a follow-up skin exam for my perioral dermatitis today.\n\nDoctor: Okay, and how is your perioral dermatitis now?\n\nPatient: It\'s well-controlled, but it has not fully resolved. I was applying doxycycline twice a day with improvement, but my pharmacist advised me to reduce to once daily since it had started to improve, so for the past week, I have been using the doxycycline only once per day. It has not seen continued improvement, but it has not worsened.\n\nDoctor: I see. And how is your skin moisturization?\n\nPatient: It\'s much improved. I\'m using the sulfacetamide wash twice daily, and I apply MetroCream immediately after using the sulfacetamide wash. I haven\'t used any other lotions or products, Cetaphil lotion or soap, or Neutrogena wipes.\n\nDoctor: That\'s good. And what about Elidel?\n\nPatient: I didn\'t start using Elidel because it\'s not covered by my insurance, and I was concerned about possible side effects from the medication.\n\nDoctor: I understand. Let\'s take a look at your physical exam. On inspection of your skin, there are a few very faint erythematous papules with mild redness around and underneath the nostrils. Your Fitzpatrick skin type is 4 to 5.\n\nPatient: Hmm, okay.\n\nDoctor: Based on your history and physical exam, I have diagnosed you with perioral dermatitis and rosacea, including ocular rosacea.\n\nPatient: Yes, I have been diagnosed with that before.\n\nDoctor: I recommend that you resume using the doxycycline twice daily until you reach full resolution, and then reduce the application to once a day for an additional week. I understand that the cost of Elidel was too expensive for you, so I have provided a prescription for Tacrolimus. I have also counseled you on possible side effects of using the medication.\n\nPatient: Okay, I understand.\n\nDoctor: You will continue to use the sulfacetamide face wash once a day and the Bruder mask as needed. For your rosacea, including ocular rosacea, continue using the MetroCream twice daily. Gently wash your eyelids once daily with a gentle cleanser and try a gentle cream such as Vanicream.\n\nPatient: Okay, I will do that.\n\nDoctor: If your symptoms worsen, please call the clinic. You will have a follow-up appointment in 4 weeks.\n\nPatient: Okay, I will. Thank you, doctor.\n\nDoctor: You\'re welcome. Take care.\n\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\n[doctor] uh , mrn49282721 . patient\'s name is jacqueline miller . use last visit exam where appropriate .\n[doctor] 