[92mlitellm.acompletion(model='gpt-4o', api_base=None, messages=[{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee injury.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nScott Miller is a pleasant 36-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right knee injury. The injury occurred 3 days ago, when he was playing pickup soccer games and felt a pop in his right knee. He reports swelling at the time of the injury, which has improved. The patient states that his knee is not as functional or moving as well as it used to be. He rates his pain level as a 6 to 7 out of 10 currently and a 4 to 5 out of 10 when the swelling decreases. The patient has been taking ibuprofen on a regular basis, which provides some relief. He states that he is able to bend his knee with pain towards the end of this motion; however, when he goes to straighten his knee, he feels like he has to help it straight. The patient adds that he is able to bear weight on his right knee, however it feels loose and does not feel normal.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient works at a desk.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nGAIT: Marked limp in the right leg upon ambulation.\nMSK: Examination of the right knee: Significant effusion. Slight ecchymosis. Pain with palpation at the medial joint line. Difficulty with passive ROM. Lacking a few degrees of terminal extension to 115 degrees. Lacking 20-30 degrees of terminal flexion secondary to pain. Positive Lachman\'s test. Muscle strength is 5/5. Neurovascular intact distally.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee pain, possible anterior cruciate ligament tear.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have recommended that we obtain an MRI of the right knee to evaluate the integrity of the ACL. I have prescribed the patient meloxicam 15 mg a day to reduce swelling and pain. We discussed that the patient should utilize ice and activity modification to reduce swelling and pain. I recommended that he wears a knee immobilizer and use crutches when he is ambulating. He will avoid jogging until we have the results of his MRI.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me once the MRI results are available for review and further discussion.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] Hi Scott, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient] Hi, I\'m doing okay, thanks. How about you?\n\n[doctor] I\'m doing well, thank you. So, I see you\'re here for a right knee injury. Can you tell me a bit more about what happened?\n\n[patient] Sure, it happened a few days ago. I was playing in a pickup soccer game and I felt a pop in my right knee. It was pretty painful and swollen right after.\n\n[doctor] I see. How has it been since then? Has the swelling improved at all?\n\n[patient] Yes, the swelling has gone down a bit, but my knee still isn\'t moving as well as it used to. It hurts quite a bit, especially when I try to straighten it.\n\n[doctor] Hmm, that sounds uncomfortable. On a scale of 1 to 10, how would you rate your pain right now?\n\n[patient] I\'d say it\'s about a 6 or 7 out of 10.\n\n[doctor] And how about when the swelling is less? Does the pain change then?\n\n[patient] Yeah, when the swelling goes down, the pain goes down too. It\'s more like a 4 to 5 out of 10 then.\n\n[doctor] Okay, that\'s helpful to know. Have you been taking anything for the pain?\n\n[patient] Yes, I\'ve been taking ibuprofen regularly, and it helps a bit.\n\n[doctor] Good to know. Can you bend your knee for me?\n\n[patient] Sure, but it hurts towards the end of the motion.\n\n[doctor] I see. And how about when you try to straighten it?\n\n[patient] It feels like I have to help it straighten. It doesn\'t go all the way on its own.\n\n[doctor] Okay, and are you able to bear weight on it?\n\n[patient] Yes, I can, but it feels loose and not normal.\n\n[doctor] Alright. Let\'s talk a bit about your lifestyle. What do you do for work?\n\n[patient] I work at a desk.\n\n[doctor] Got it. Do you play soccer regularly?\n\n[patient] Yes, I do. I play in a few leagues and also pickup games.\n\n[doctor] Understood. Now, let\'s do a physical exam. I\'ll need you to walk for me. \n\n[patient] Okay.\n\n[doctor] I see you have a marked limp in your right leg when you walk. Let\'s take a closer look at your knee. I\'ll start with examining the right knee. There is significant effusion and slight ecchymosis. Does it hurt when I press here?\n\n[patient] Yes, it does.\n\n[doctor] How about here?\n\n[patient] Yes, that hurts too.\n\n[doctor] Okay, I see there\'s pain with palpation at the medial joint line. Let\'s check your range of motion. Can you bend your knee for me?\n\n[patient] Sure, but it hurts towards the end.\n\n[doctor] I can see that. You lack a few degrees of terminal extension to 115 degrees and lacking 20-30 degrees of terminal flexion due to pain. Let\'s do a Lachman\'s test. I\'ll move your knee like this. Does that hurt?\n\n[patient] Yes, it does.\n\n[doctor] Alright, that\'s a positive Lachman\'s test. How about muscle strength? Can you push against my hand?\n\n[patient] Sure.\n\n[doctor] Good, your muscle strength is 5/5. Now, let\'s check your neurovascular status. Do you feel this?\n\n[patient] Yes, I can feel that.\n\n[doctor] Great, your neurovascular status is intact distally. Based on your symptoms and the physical exam, I\'m concerned you might have an anterior cruciate ligament tear. We need to get an MRI of your right knee to confirm this.\n\n[patient] Okay, that makes sense.\n\n[doctor] In the meantime, I\'ll prescribe meloxicam 15 mg a day to help with the swelling and pain. Make sure to use ice and modify your activity to reduce swelling and pain. Wear a knee immobilizer and use crutches when you\'re walking. Avoid jogging until we get the MRI results.\n\n[patient] Got it. How long will it take to get the MRI results?\n\n[doctor] It should be ready within a few days. We\'ll schedule a follow-up appointment once we have the results for further discussion.\n\n[patient] Alright, thank you.\n\n[doctor] You\'re welcome. Do you have any other questions for me?\n\n[patient] No, I think that\'s it for now.\n\n[doctor] Okay, take care and we\'ll see you soon. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Hello Scott, how are you feeling today?\nPatient: I\'m doing okay, thanks for asking.\nDoctor: I see from your chief complaint that you have a right knee injury. Can you tell me more about that?\nPatient: Yeah, I was playing pickup soccer games a few days ago and felt a pop in my right knee. I had swelling at the time and it\'s improved, but my knee isn\'t moving as well as it used to.\nDoctor: I see. H