-d '{'model': 'gpt-4o', 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTeresa Sullivan is a pleasant 33-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right elbow pain. Her pain began 4 days ago, without any improvement. She denies any specific injury; however, she has recently increased her usage of a pottery wheel in creating a bowl for her son. She is currently unable to work with the pottery wheel secondary to pain. Her pain is in the medial aspect of her elbow. She notes it radiates medially down her arm as well. The patient rates her pain level as 7 out of 10 at its worst. She reports the pain wakes her at night if she rolls over. She denies ibuprofen or ice provide pain relief. The patient denies any previous elbow injuries.\n\nThe patient has a history of hypertension. She states she has been checking her blood pressures regularly. She adds that her blood pressures have been in the 110s to 130s range. She affirms she is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and denies missing doses.\n\nRegarding her diabetes type 2, she reports she sometimes forgets to take metformin 500 mg twice daily. At times, her blood sugar levels range from 150 to 170 mg/dL. She states that she has a sweet tooth and indulges in home baked goods. For exercise, she walks in the evening and in the morning. She walks 1 to 2 miles per day around her neighborhood.\n\nThe patient enjoys working with ceramics as well as baking cakes and cinnamon rolls for family gatherings.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of hypertension and diabetes type 2.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking lisinopril 10 mg and metformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 120/70 mmHg.\nPulse Rate: Appropriate.\nRespiratory Rate: 18 breaths per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 100% on room air.\nBody Temperature: 97.9 degrees F.\nPain Level: 7/10 at its worst.\nWeight: 5 lb increase since last visit.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: No murmurs, gallops or rubs. Pulses are equal bilaterally.\nRESPIRATORY: Normal respiratory effort, no respiratory distress. Lungs are clear bilaterally to auscultation.\nSKIN: No rash or lesions.\nMSK: Examination of the right elbow: Moderate tenderness at the medial epicondyle. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. Pain with flexion and bending the right wrist. No bruising or swelling. No discoloration or lesions.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAn x-ray of the right elbow was reviewed today. It demonstrated no evidence of any fractures. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Right elbow pain, consistent with medial epicondylitis.\n2. Diabetes type 2.\n3. Hypertension.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with her regarding her current symptoms. I have explained to her that her elbow pain is consistent with medial epicondylitis and was most likely caused by overuse and damage to the tendons in her arm. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The patient will wear a sling on her right arm while she is awake to provide increased support. I advised her to ice her right elbow for 20 minutes 3 times per day. She will take ibuprofen 600 mg every 6 hours with food for 1 week. I have also advised her to hold off on creating pottery for the next 2 weeks to allow her arm to heal. If the patient continues to experience persistent elbow pain, we may consider formal physical therapy at her next visit.\n\nI have encouraged the patient to check and record her blood sugars every day. I will increase her metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. The patient and I discussed the importance of her taking her medication on a regular basis twice a day. I have also recommended that she be referred to diabetic counseling for more information on the steps she can take to manage her diabetes. I will also order a hemoglobin A1c test for a more accurate assessment of her long-term blood sugar levels.\n\nThe patient’s hypertension is currently controlled with medication and exercise. I have instructed her to continue taking lisinopril 10 mg. I also encouraged her to continue with her walking routine.\n\nAll questions were answered.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to review the results of the hemoglobin A1c test and discuss her progress.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Good morning, Teresa. How are you doing today?\n\n[patient]: Good morning, Doctor. I\'ve been better. I\'m here because of this elbow pain.\n\n[doctor]: I see. Can you tell me more about this elbow pain? When did it start?\n\n[patient]: It started about 4 days ago. It just hasn\'t gotten any better.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, I understand. Did you injure your elbow recently, or do you remember doing anything that might have caused this pain?\n\n[patient]: I haven\'t had any specific injury, but I have been using a pottery wheel a lot more lately to make a bowl for my son. Maybe that has something to do with it.\n\n[doctor]: That could be a possible cause. Are you able to work with the pottery wheel right now?\n\n[patient]: No, I can\'t. The pain is just too much.\n\n[doctor]: I see. Can you describe the pain for me? Is it sharp, dull, throbbing?\n\n[patient]: It\'s more of a sharp pain, and it radiates down my arm, especially towards the inside.\n\n[doctor]: How would you rate your pain on a scale from 1 to 10, with 10 being the worst pain you\'ve ever felt?\n\n[patient]: I\'d say it\'s about a 7 at its worst.\n\n[doctor]: Does the pain wake you up at night?\n\n[patient]: Yes, it does. If I roll over the wrong way, it wakes me up.\n\n[doctor]: Have you tried anything to relieve the pain, like ibuprofen or ice?\n\n[patient]: I\'ve tried both, but neither seems to help.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. Have you had any previous elbow injuries?\n\n[patient]: No, I haven\'t had any elbow injuries before.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. Let\'s talk a bit about your medical history. Do you have any chronic conditions?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I have hypertension and type 2 diabetes.\n\n[doctor]: Are you taking any medications for these conditions?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I take lisinopril 10 mg daily for my blood pressure and metformin 500 mg twice a day for my diabetes.\n\n[doctor]: Have you been consistent with taking your medications?\n\n[patient]: I try to, but I sometimes forget to take the metformin.\n\n[doctor]: How often do you forget to take it?\n\n[patient]: Maybe a couple of times a week.\n\n[doctor]: And how are your blood sugar levels? Have you been checking them regularly?\n\n[patient]: I do check them, but sometimes they\'re a bit high. They usually range from 150 to 170 mg/dL.\n\n[doctor]: I see. Do you have a sweet tooth or indulge in sugary foods?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I do have a sweet tooth. I love baking cakes and cinnamon rolls for family gatherings.\n\n[doctor]: That can definitely affect your blood sugar levels. How about exercise? Do you have a regular routine?\n\n[patient]: I walk in the evening and morning, about 1 to 2 miles per day around my neighborhood.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s good to hear. Now, let\'s review your vitals. Your blood pressure is 120/70 mmHg, pulse rate is appropriate, respiratory rate is 18 breaths per minute, oxygen saturation is 100% on room air, and your temperature is 97.9 degrees F. Your pain level is 7 out of 10 at its worst. Have you noticed any weight changes?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I\'ve gained about 5 pounds since my last visit.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, let\'s move on to the physical exam. I\'ll start with your cardiovascular system. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. Pulses are equal bilaterally. Your respiratory system shows normal respiratory effort, no respiratory distress, and lungs are clear bilaterally to auscultation. Your skin looks fine, no rash or lesions. Now, let\'s examine your right elbow. There\'s moderate tenderness at the medial epicondyle, pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm, and pain with flexion and bending of the right wrist. No bruising or swelling, and no discoloration or lesions. \n\n[patient]: Okay.\n\n[doctor]: I reviewed the x-ray of your right elbow. There\'s no evidence of any fractures or other abnormalities. Based on your symptoms and the exam, it looks like you have medial epicondylitis, also known as golfer\'s elbow.\n\n[patient]: Oh, I see. What can we do about it?\n\n[doctor]: We\'ll treat it conservatively. I recommend you wear a sling on your right arm while you\'re awake to provide increased support. Ice your right elbow for 20 minutes three times a day. Take ibuprofen 600 mg every 6 hours with food for one week. Also, try to avoid creating pottery for the next two weeks to allow your arm to heal.\n\n[patient]: Alright, I can do that.\n\n[doctor]: I also noticed your blood pressure has been in the 110s to 130s range, which is good. Continue taking your lisinopril 10 mg daily. For your diabetes, it\'s important to take your metformin regularly. I\'m going to increase your dose to 1000 mg twice a day. We\'ll also need to check your hemoglobin A1c for a more accurate assessment of your long-term blood sugar levels.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I understand.\n\n[doctor]: Lastly, I recommend you see a diabetic counselor for more information on managing your diabetes. It\'s crucial to follow a consistent diet and exercise routine. Continue your walking routine as well.\n\n[patient]: Got it. I\'ll do that.\n\n[doctor]: Do you have any questions or concerns?\n\n[patient]: No, I think that\'s everything.\n\n[doctor]: Great. We\'ll follow up in two weeks to review the results of the hemoglobin A1c test and discuss your progress. If the pain persists, we might consider formal physical therapy at your next visit.\n\n[patient]: Thank you, Doctor.\n\n[doctor]: You\'re welcome. Take care and see you in two weeks.\n\n[patient]: Thank you. Bye.\n\n[doctor]: Bye. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Good morning, how are you feeling today?\nPatient: Good morning, I\'m feeling okay, but I have some pain in my right elbow.\nDoctor: Can you tell me more about your pain? When did it start?\nPatient: It started 4 days ago and it\'s still there. I can\'t use my pottery wheel because of it.\nDoctor: I see. Have you had any injury to your right elbow recently?\nPatient: No, I haven\'t had any injury, but I\'ve been using my pottery wheel more often.\nDoctor: Hmm, I understand. Can you describe the pain for me?\nPatient: The pain is in the inside of my elbow and it radiates down my arm.\nDoctor: How would you rate your pain on a scale of 1 to 10?\nPatient: I would rate it 7 out of 10.\nDoctor: I see. Does anything help with the pain, lik