-d '{'model': 'gpt-4o', 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient\'s family owns and runs a bakery.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking metformin twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports back pain.\nNeurological: Denies numbness or tingling in feet.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nEyes\n- Examination: No redness, discharge, or icterus.\n\nEars, Nose, Mouth, and Throat\n- Examination of Ears: Auditory canal and tympanic membranes are clear.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm.\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: No pain to palpation of the upper back. Pain to palpation of the midline of the lower back, especially on the left. No pain to palpation on the right lower back. Mildly positive straight leg rise on the right Positive straight leg rise on the left.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Low back pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: I believe he has a musculoskeletal low back strain. I do not expect to see anything significant on x-ray, but we will obtain one for further evaluation of any possible disc issues.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed his diagnosis at length today. I explained that physical therapy can help with the pain, but also strengthen some of the muscles in the back. I explained that he needs to be careful while taking medications, such as an overuse of muscle relaxers if he is doing any strenuous or hazardous activities such as driving.\n- Medical Treatment: Low back x-ray ordered. Referral to physical therapy provided. He will take 400 mg of ibuprofen up to 3 times per day. Tylenol can be used for severe pain. Prescription for Flexeril also provided.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: His diabetes is currently under control.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of maintaining a healthy lifestyle. I encouraged him to try an avoid foods high in sugar. He asked many questions and I feel he is confident in the plan at this point.\n- Medical Treatment: Hemoglobin A1c ordered as he has not had an updated one in almost 5 months. He will continue with his diet and exercise. Continue current dose of metformin for the time being. The patient will be checking his blood sugar in the morning and will log this information for me.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Good morning! How are you feeling today?\n\n[patient]: Good morning, doctor. I\'ve been better. I\'ve been having some back pain.\n\n[doctor]: I see. Can you describe the back pain for me? Where exactly is it located?\n\n[patient]: It\'s mainly in my lower back, around the middle. It feels pretty bad when I move or bend down.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, I understand. How long have you been experiencing this pain?\n\n[patient]: It\'s been about a week now.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. Have you noticed if anything makes the pain better or worse?\n\n[patient]: Moving around and bending down definitely makes it worse. Resting seems to help a bit.\n\n[doctor]: Got it. Have you tried any medications or treatments to relieve the pain?\n\n[patient]: I\'ve been taking some over-the-counter painkillers, but they don\'t seem to help much.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. Can you tell me about your medical history? Any chronic conditions or medications you\'re currently taking?\n\n[patient]: I have diabetes. I take metformin twice daily.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, thanks for letting me know. Do you have any numbness or tingling in your feet?\n\n[patient]: No, I haven\'t noticed any numbness or tingling.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. How about your social habits? What do you do for work?\n\n[patient]: My family owns and runs a bakery. I help out there.\n\n[doctor]: That sounds interesting. Do you get much physical activity at work?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I move around a lot, but it\'s mostly light stuff, like carrying small items and baking.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, that\'s good to know. Let\'s go ahead and do a physical exam now. I\'ll start with your eyes. \n\n[patient]: Sure.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, your eyes look fine. No redness, discharge, or icterus. Now, let\'s check your ears. Your auditory canals and tympanic membranes are clear. Let\'s move on to your lungs. I\'ll listen to your breathing now.\n\n[patient]: Okay.\n\n[doctor]: Your lungs sound clear bilaterally. Next, I\'ll listen to your heart. \n\n[patient]: Alright.\n\n[doctor]: Your heart rate and rhythm are regular. Now, let\'s examine your back. I\'ll start with your upper back. Any pain when I press here?\n\n[patient]: No, that feels fine.\n\n[doctor]: How about the midline of your lower back?\n\n[patient]: Yes, that hurts, especially on the left side.\n\n[doctor]: I see. And what about the right side?\n\n[patient]: No pain there.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, I\'ll check your straight leg raise now. Lift your right leg while keeping it straight. Does this cause any pain?\n\n[patient]: Yes, it does.\n\n[doctor]: And now the left leg?\n\n[patient]: Yes, that hurts too.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, thank you for that. Based on your symptoms and physical exam, it seems like you have a musculoskeletal low back strain. I don\'t expect to see anything significant on an x-ray, but we\'ll get one to rule out any possible disc issues.\n\n[patient]: Alright, that sounds good.\n\n[doctor]: For your back pain, I recommend physical therapy. It can help with the pain and strengthen some of the muscles in your back. I\'ll also prescribe you 400 mg of ibuprofen to take up to three times a day. For severe pain, you can use Tylenol.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I can do that.\n\n[doctor]: I\'ll also prescribe Flexeril for muscle relaxation. Just be careful with these medications, especially if you\'re doing any strenuous or hazardous activities like driving.\n\n[patient]: Got it.\n\n[doctor]: Now, let\'s talk about your diabetes. How have you been managing it?\n\n[patient]: I\'ve been trying to keep up with my diet and exercise.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s good. It\'s important to maintain a healthy lifestyle. Have you had your Hemoglobin A1c checked recently?\n\n[patient]: Not in a while, almost five months I think.\n\n[doctor]: We\'ll order a new Hemoglobin A1c test to see how your diabetes is doing. Continue with your current dose of metformin and keep checking your blood sugar in the morning. Log this information for me, please.\n\n[patient]: Will do.\n\n[doctor]: Do you have any questions or concerns about your treatment plan?\n\n[patient]: No, I think I understand everything.\n\n[doctor]: Great. We\'ll get that x-ray ordered and set up your referral to physical therapy. Continue with your current medications and lifestyle changes. If you have any issues or questions, feel free to contact us.\n\n[patient]: Thank you, doctor.\n\n[doctor]: You\'re welcome. Take care and see you soon.\n\n[patient]: Thanks, you too. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ \nDoctor: Good morning, what brings you in today?\n\nPatient: I have been having back pain.\n\nDoctor: I see. Can you tell me a little more about your history?\n\nPatient: I have a history of diabetes.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And what do you do for a living?\n\nPatient: My family runs a bakery.\n\nDoctor: I see. What medications are you taking?\n\nPatient: I take metformin twice a day.\n\nDoctor: Alright. Let\'s take a look at your back pain. Can you describe the pain for me?\n\nPatient: It\'s in my lower back, especially on the left side.\n\nDoctor: Hmm, I see. And have you experienced any numbness or tingling in your feet?\n\nPatient: No, I haven\'t.\n\nDoctor: Alright. Let\'s do a physical exam. Can you tell me if you have any redness, discharge, or icterus in your eyes?\n\nPatient: No, I don\'t have any of those.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And how about your ears?\n\nPatient: The auditory canal and tympanic membranes are clear.\n\nDoctor: Great. And how about your respiratory system? Auscultation of your lungs, are they clear?\n\nPatient: Yes, they are.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And how about your cardiovascular system? Auscultation of your heart, is it regular and rhythmic?\n\nPatient: Yes, it is.\n\nDoctor: Alright. Let\'s take a look at your back now. Can you tell me if you feel any pain when I press on your upper back?\n\nPatient: No, I don\'t feel any pain there. But I do feel pain when I press on the midline of my lower back, especially on the left side.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And how about your right lower back?\n\nPatient: There\'s no pain there.\n\nDoctor: Alright. And can you lift your legs straight up without any pain?\n\nPatient: Yes, I can on the right, but not on the left.\n\nDoctor: Alright. Based on your symptoms and examination, I believe you have a low back strain. I don\'t expect to see anything significant on your x-ray, but we will obtain one for further evaluation of any possible disc issues.\n\nPatient: Okay.\n\nDoctor: I have referred you to physical therapy to help with the pain and strengthen some of the muscles in your back. And I have prescribed ibuprofen for you to take up to three times a day, and Tylenol for severe pain. I have also prescribed Flexeril for you.\n\nPatient: Okay.\n\nDoctor: And I would like to check your Hemoglobin A1c as you haven\'t had an updated one in almost 5 months. You will continue with your diet and exercise and continue taking your metformin for the time being. Can you check your blood sugar in the morning and log this information for me?\n\nPatient: Sure, I can do that.\n\nDoctor: Great. And I would like to emphasize the importance of maintaining a healthy lifestyle and avoiding foods high in sugar.\n\nPatient: Okay, I understand.\n\nDoctor: Alright, if you have any questions or concerns, feel free to ask.\n\nPatient: No, I think I understand everything. Thank you, doctor.\n\nDoctor: You\'re welcome. Take care.\n\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\n[doctor] good morning noah tell me a little bit about why you are here today\n[patient] hi document i\'m i\'m just had you know the back pain you know and i\'ve i\'ve had it for you know about a week and\n[doctor] oh\n[patient] you know i mean i tried some you know i mean medications but you know some over the counter stuff but it does n\'t go away\n[doctor] okay can you tell me a little bit more about it how did it start or how did it do you remember injuring yourself\n[patient] no not really i mean i was just like you know i guess i one day i mean i just twisted you know on my left and maybe i mean i might have picked something up\n[doctor] okay\n[patient] so i mean at that time i mean there was this pain but i you know it went away you know and i thought that was 