that he runs 5 days per week. He adds that he alternates between a long run and a long bike ride on weekends. He currently takes one day off from his exercises. The patient states that he does not stretch at all. He denies any left leg pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right heel pain. Denies left leg pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right heel: Pain with resisted extension. Mild pain with flexion. Positive calcaneal squeeze test. Severe tenderness to the Achilles tendon. Thompson test is negative, bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nNo new imaging was obtained today\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight Achilles tendinitis.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination today, I had a lengthy discussion with the patient regarding his current symptoms. I have explained to him that his symptoms are likely secondary to overuse. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The patient was instructed to take at least 1 week off from his training to recover from this. I will provide him with a referral for formal physical therapy to strengthen his right lower extremities. I have advised him to take 3 tablets of ibuprofen every 6 hours for pain. If his pain does not improve with physical therapy, I will recommend obtaining an x-ray.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 7 to 10 days to check on his progress.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] Hi Dennis, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient] Hi, Doctor. I\'m doing okay, but I\'m here because of this pain in my right heel.\n\n[doctor] I see. Can you tell me more about the pain you\'re experiencing?\n\n[patient] Sure. The pain started about 10 days ago while I was training for a triathlon. It\'s on the back of my heel and sometimes it goes up into my calf. It gets worse when I work out and when I get up in the morning. If I sit for a long time and then stand up, it hurts again.\n\n[doctor] Hmm, that sounds uncomfortable. How would you describe the pain? Is it sharp, dull, throbbing?\n\n[patient] It\'s more of a dull ache, but it can get pretty sharp sometimes, especially when I\'m working out.\n\n[doctor] And you mentioned it gets worse with exercise. How often do you exercise?\n\n[patient] I run about 10 kilometers every morning, and then at lunch, I try to get on the bike for about 45 minutes to an hour. In the evening, I go to the aquatic center and swim for 30 minutes to an hour.\n\n[doctor] That’s quite a lot of exercise. Do you take any days off from your routine?\n\n[patient] I usually take one day off a week.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Do you stretch before or after your workouts?\n\n[patient] No, I don’t really stretch at all.\n\n[doctor] Alright. Do you have any pain in your left leg?\n\n[patient] No, just the right heel.\n\n[doctor] Got it. Let’s talk a bit about your lifestyle. Do you have any other medical conditions or take any medications?\n\n[patient] No, I don\'t have any other medical conditions and I\'m not on any medications.\n\n[doctor] Okay, let’s move on to the physical exam. I’m going to check your heel and calf. Let me know if you feel any pain. \n\n[patient] Sure, go ahead.\n\n[doctor] (Examining) I see. You have pain with resisted extension and mild pain with flexion. There is severe tenderness to the Achilles tendon. Let’s do the calcaneal squeeze test. Does this hurt?\n\n[patient] Yes, it does.\n\n[doctor] And the Thompson test is negative on both sides. Based on your symptoms and the physical exam, it looks like you have Achilles tendinitis. \n\n[patient] Oh, I see. What does that mean?\n\n[doctor] Achilles tendinitis is an inflammation of the Achilles tendon, which connects your calf muscles to your heel bone. It’s often caused by overuse, especially in people who do a lot of high-impact activities like running and jumping.\n\n[patient] That makes sense. What can we do about it?\n\n[doctor] First, I recommend that you take at least one week off from your training to allow your body to recover. I’ll also refer you to physical therapy to strengthen your right lower extremities. \n\n[patient] Okay, I can do that.\n\n[doctor] For pain relief, you can take 3 tablets of ibuprofen every 6 hours. It should help reduce the inflammation and pain.\n\n[patient] Alright, I’ll do that.\n\n[doctor] If your pain doesn’t improve with physical therapy, we may need to get an x-ray to rule out any other issues. \n\n[patient] Got it. How long should I wait before coming back for a follow-up?\n\n[doctor] I’d like to see you back in 7 to 10 days to check on your progress. \n\n[patient] Okay, I’ll make sure to schedule that.\n\n[doctor] Great. Do you have any other questions or concerns?\n\n[patient] No, I think that covers everything. Thank you, Doctor.\n\n[doctor] You’re welcome, Dennis. Take care and I’ll see you in a few days.\n\n[patient] Thanks, Doctor. See you then. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ \nDoctor: Good morning, Dennis. How are you feeling today?\n\nPatient: I\'m feeling okay, thank you.\n\nDoctor: I understand you\'re here today for an evaluation of your right heel pain. Can you tell me more about your symptoms?\n\nPatient: Yes, I\'ve been having pain in my right heel for about 10 days now. It started while I was training for a triathlon.\n\nDoctor: Hmm, I see. Can you describe the pain for me? Where is it located exactly?\n\nPatient: It\'s located on the back of my heel and it radiates up into my calf.\n\nDoctor: I see. Does the pain get worse when you work out or when you first get up in the morning?\n\nPatient: Yes, it does. And if I sit for a long time, it comes back when I stand up.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And how often do you exercise?\n\nPatient: I run 10 kilometers in the morning, then I go for a 45-minute to 1-hour bike ride at lunchtime, and then I swim for 30 minutes to 1 hour in the evening. I run 5 days a week and take one day off. I don\'t stretch at all.\n\nDoctor: I see. And do you have any pain in your left leg?\n\nPatient: No, I don\'