[92mlitellm.acompletion(model='gpt-4o', api_base=None, messages=[{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow up status post inferior STEMI.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nRCA stent 2018\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\naspirin 81 mg daily\nBrilinta 90 mg twice daily\nLipitor 80 mg daily\nToprol 50 mg daily\nlisinopril 20 mg daily\nmetformin\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral leg swelling.\n\nVITALS\n\nWithin normal limits\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No jugular venous distention.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: No carotid bruits. A 3/6 systolic ejection murmur is heard at the left base.\n- Examination: Right radial artery cath site is clean, dry, and intact without hematoma. Radial artery pulses are palpable.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Bilateral lower limb edema. Pitting 1+.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG is reviewed and demonstrates a normal sinus rhythm. Good R wave progression. Evolutionary changes that are anticipated after a STEMI.\n\nEchocardiogram is reviewed and demonstrates a reduced ejection fraction of 35%.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of coronary artery disease that we will continue to monitor.\n- Medical Treatment: She will continue aspirin 81 mg daily and Brilinta 90 mg twice daily. She will continue Lipitor 80 mg a day and Toprol 50 mg daily. I am going to refer her to cardiac rehab for education and to gain confidence to resume exercising regularly.\n\n2. Newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation.\n- Medical Reasoning: A recent echocardiogram revealed a reduced ejection fraction of 35%. She was able to be treated in the cath lab in a timely manner, so I think that the muscle is just stunned. Given her history. it will improve over time.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that she will recover since she is compliant with her medications.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Acute heart failure.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is retaining fluid.\n- Medical Treatment: I am going to prescribe Lasix 40 mg once a day. I want to add Aldactone 12.5 mg daily. I will order lab work. We will obtain a repeat echo in approximately 2 months.\n\n4. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: Blood pressure is well controlled. She will tolerate the Aldactone well.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to continue exercising and to be cognizant of her diet. I explained that there are healthier alternatives available to reduce the amount of preserved meats she consumes.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 20 mg daily. The patient received a referral to nutrition counseling to consider what other diet options might be available to her.\n\n5. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: Stable.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to continue monitoring her blood glucose levels.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue metformin as prescribed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up as instructed.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] Hi there, how are you feeling today?\n\n[patient] Hi, I\'m doing okay, thank you.\n\n[doctor] Great to hear. I\'m Dr. Smith. I see you\'re here for a follow-up after your recent inferior STEMI. How have you been feeling since then?\n\n[patient] I\'ve been feeling alright, no major issues. Just a bit worried about everything.\n\n[doctor] Understandable. Let\'s go over your history a bit. You had a stent placed in 2018 for a RCA blockage, correct?\n\n[patient] Yes, that\'s right.\n\n[doctor] And you\'re currently taking aspirin 81 mg daily, Brilinta 90 mg twice daily, Lipitor 80 mg daily, Toprol 50 mg daily, lisinopril 20 mg daily, and metformin for your diabetes, correct?\n\n[patient] Yes, that\'s all correct.\n\n[doctor] Good. Have you experienced any chest pain recently?\n\n[patient] No, no chest pain at all.\n\n[doctor] That\'s good to hear. Any shortness of breath?\n\n[patient] No, I haven\'t had any shortness of breath either.\n\n[doctor] How about swelling in your legs?\n\n[patient] Yes, I\'ve noticed some swelling in both my legs.\n\n[doctor] Alright, let\'s check your vitals. Everything looks within normal limits. Let me do a quick physical exam. \n\n[doctor] I\'m going to check your neck first. No jugular venous distention. Now, I\'ll listen to your lungs. Your lungs are clear bilaterally. \n\n[doctor] Let\'s listen to your heart. I hear a 3/6 systolic ejection murmur at the left base. Your radial artery cath site looks clean, dry, and intact without hematoma. Radial artery pulses are palpable. \n\n[doctor] Now, let\'s check your legs. I see some bilateral lower limb edema with pitting 1+. \n\n[doctor] I reviewed your EKG, and it shows a normal sinus rhythm with good R wave progression, which is expected after a STEMI. Your echocardiogram shows a reduced ejection fraction of 35%. \n\n[doctor] Based on your history and exam, here\'s what we\'ll do. First, for your coronary artery disease, you\'ll continue your current medications: aspirin 81 mg daily and Brilinta 90 mg twice daily, along with Lipitor 80 mg daily and Toprol 50 mg daily. I\'ll also refer you to cardiac rehab to help you regain confidence and learn how to exercise safely.\n\n[patient] Okay, that sounds good.\n\n[doctor] For your reduced left ventricular function and moderate mitral regurgitation, your recent echocardiogram shows a reduced ejection fraction of 35%. This is likely due to the muscle being "stunned" from your STEMI, but it should improve over time since you\'re compliant with your medications.\n\n[patient] That\'s reassuring to hear.\n\n[doctor] Continue taking lisinopril 20 mg daily. Now, regarding your fluid retention, I\'m going to prescribe Lasix 40 mg once a day and add Aldactone 12.5 mg daily. We\'ll also do some lab work and a repeat echo in about two months.\n\n[patient] Alright, I\'ll do that.\n\n[doctor] Your blood pressure is well controlled, so you can continue with lisinopril 20 mg daily. I also encourage you to continue exercising and be mindful of your diet. There are healthier alternatives to reduce the amount of preserved meats. I\'ll refer you to nutrition counseling to explore these options.\n\n[patient] Okay, I\'ll look into that.\n\n[doctor] Lastly, for your type 2 diabetes, continue monitoring your blood glucose levels as you have been. \n\n[patient] Will do.\n\n[doctor] Do you have any questions or concerns about the treatment plan?\n\n[patient] No, everything sounds clear. Thank you.\n\n[doctor] Great. We\'ll see you back in two months for a follow-up, and we\'ll repeat the echocardiogram then. If you have any issues before then, don\'t hesitate to contact us.\n\n[patient] Thank you, Doctor.\n\n[doctor] You\'re welcome. Take care and see you soon.\n\n[patient] Thanks, you too. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Good morning, how are you feeling today?\nPatient: I\'m feeling okay, thanks.\nDoctor: That\'s great to hear. So, I see that you\'re here for a follow-up status post inferior STEMI. Can you tell me if you\'ve had any chest pain recently?\nPatient: No, I haven\'t had any chest pain.\nDoctor: Okay, that\'s good to hear. I also see that you have a history of coronary artery disease. We\'ll continue to monitor that. You\'ll need to keep taking your aspirin, Brilinta, Lipitor, Toprol, and lisinopril daily.\nPatient: Okay, I will.\nDoctor: I also noticed that you have bilateral leg swelling. Have you noticed any changes in your breathing or had any shortness of breath?\nPatient: No, I haven\'t had any issues with breathing.\nDoctor: Great. Your vitals are within normal limits and your physical exam was also normal. Your EKG showed a normal sinus rhythm with good R wave progression. Your echocardiogram showed a reduced ejection fraction of 35%, but given your recent treatment for your STEMI, I think your muscle is just stunned and should improve over time.\nPatient: Okay, that\'s good to hear.\nDoctor: Yes, I advised you that you will recover since you are compliant with your medications. We\'ll continue with your current medical treatment plan and I\'m going to refer you to cardiac rehab for education and to gain confidence to resume exercising regularly.\nPatient: Okay, that sounds good.\nDoctor: I also noticed that you have acute heart failure and hypertension. We\'ll prescribe Lasix and Aldactone to help with fluid retention and blood pressure control. We\'ll also order lab work and a rep