 patient reports that she works on a keyboard all day.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nThe patient reports that she has been taking ibuprofen.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies sleep disturbance.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports pain in the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd digits on the right hand.\nNeurological: Reports numbness and tingling in the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd digits on the right hand.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right hand: Pain with flexion and extension of the wrist.. Decreased grip strength on the right compared to the left. Positive Tinel\'s sign.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight carpal tunnel syndrome.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing her symptoms and exam findings, I believe her symptoms are secondary to carpal tunnel syndrome. We discussed the nature of this condition, as well as her treatment options, in detail. I recommend we treat this conservatively with nighttime wrist splinting and ibuprofen, but I want to increase her dose to 600 mg 4 times daily. I\'m also going to order an EMG/Nerve conduction study for further evaluation. If this is positive, we can consider additional treatment with physical therapy or surgical intervention with carpal tunnel release.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] Good morning, Alexis. How are you today?\n\n[patient] Good morning, doctor. I\'m doing okay, but I\'m here because of my hand pain.\n\n[doctor] I see. Can you tell me more about the pain you\'re experiencing?\n\n[patient] Sure. About six months ago, I started having numbness and tingling in my fingers. It was on and off at first, but now it\'s constant. The pain is mostly in my 1st, 2nd, and 3rd fingers of my right hand.\n\n[doctor] Hmm, I see. Has this pain been affecting your daily activities?\n\n[patient] Yes, it has. I work on a keyboard all day, and it hurts a lot when I\'m typing or using the mouse.\n\n[doctor] That sounds very uncomfortable. Have you noticed any sleep disturbances because of the pain?\n\n[patient] No, I haven\'t had any trouble sleeping because of it.\n\n[doctor] Okay, that\'s good to know. Have you tried any treatments or medications for the pain?\n\n[patient] Yes, I\'ve been taking ibuprofen, and it helps a bit. Resting also seems to make it better.\n\n[doctor] Alright. Let\'s review your social history a bit. You mentioned you work on a keyboard all day. Is that correct?\n\n[patient] Yes, that\'s right.\n\n[doctor] And you\'re currently taking ibuprofen for the pain. Any other medications?\n\n[patient] No, just the ibuprofen.\n\n[doctor] Okay, let\'s move on to the physical exam. I\'ll need to examine your right hand. Is that alright?\n\n[patient] Sure, go ahead.\n\n[doctor] Alright, I\'m going to check the range of motion in your wrist. Please try to flex and extend your wrist for me. Does this cause any pain?\n\n[patient] Yes, it hurts when I do that.\n\n[doctor] I see. Now, I\'m going to check your grip strength. Can you squeeze my fingers as hard as you can?\n\n[patient] Okay. [squeezes]\n\n[doctor] Thank you. Your grip strength is definitely weaker on your right hand compared to your left. Let\'s check for Tinel\'s sign. I\'m going to tap on the inside of your wrist. Let me know if you feel any tingling or pain.\n\n[patient] Yes, I feel tingling.\n\n[doctor] That\'s a positive Tinel\'s sign. Based on your symptoms and the exam, it looks like you may have carpal tunnel syndrome.\n\n[patient] Oh, what does that mean?\n\n[doctor] Carpal tunnel syndrome is a condition where the median nerve is compressed at the wrist, leading to numbness, tingling, and pain in the hand and fingers. It\'s common in people who use their hands extensively, especially those who type or use a mouse frequently.\n\n[patient] That makes sense. What can we do about it?\n\n[doctor] We can start with conservative treatments. I recommend using a wrist splint at night to keep your wrist in a neutral position. I also want you to increase your ibuprofen dose to 600 mg, four times a day.\n\n[patient] Okay, I can do that.\n\n[doctor] I also want to order an EMG/Nerve conduction study to evaluate the severity of your carpal tunnel syndrome. This will help us determine if you need additional treatment like physical therapy or, in some cases, surgery.\n\n[patient] Alright, that sounds good.\n\n[doctor] Do you have any questions about the plan?\n\n[patient] No, I think I understand. \n\n[doctor] Great. If the EMG study comes back positive, we can consider additional treatments like physical therapy or even carpal tunnel release surgery. But let\'s start with the conservative approach and see how you respond.\n\n[patient] Okay, thank you, doctor.\n\n[doctor] You\'re welcome. We\'ll schedule the EMG study and I\'ll see you back in two weeks to review the results. In the meantime, start using the wrist splint and take the increased dose of ibuprofen.\n\n[patient] Got it. Thank you.\n\n[doctor] Take care, Alexis. See you in two weeks.\n\n[patient] Thanks, doctor. See you then. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Good morning, Alexis. How are you feeling today?\nAlexis: Hi, I\'m feeling okay, thanks.\nDoctor: So, you presented to the clinic today for the evaluation of your right hand pain. Can you tell me a little bit about your history?\nAlexis: Sure. About 6 months ago, I started experiencing some numbness and tingling in my right hand. It\'s been getting worse over the past month and now it\'s constant. It hurts when I type or use a mouse.\nDoctor: I see. Have you had any sleep disturbance associated with these symptoms?\nAlexis: No, I haven\'t.\nDoctor: Okay. Have you tried anything to relieve the symptoms?\nAlexis: Yes, rest and ibuprofen have been helping.\nDoctor: Alright. I see from your medical records that you work on a keyboard all day. That could be contributing to your symptoms. Have you been taking the ibuprofen regularly?\nAlexis: Yes, I have.\nDoctor: Great. During the physical exam, I noticed some pain with flexion and extension of your wrist and decreased grip strength on your right hand compared to your left. I also elicited a positive Tinel\'s sign. Based on these findings, I believe you have right carpal tunnel syndrome.\nAlexis: Okay.\nDoctor: We can treat this conservatively with nighttime wrist splinting and increasing your ibuprofen dose to 600 mg 4 times daily. I also recommend an EMG/Nerve conduction study for further evaluation. If the results are positive, we can consider additional treatment with physical therapy or surgical intervention with carpal tunnel release.\nAlexis: Okay, that sounds good.\nDoctor: Do you have any questions for me?\nAlexis: No, I think you explained everything well.\nDoctor: Alright. I\'ll write you a prescription for the ibuprofen and schedule the EMG/Nerve conduction study. Please follow up with me in a few weeks to discuss the results and your treatment plan.\nAlexis: Okay, thank you.\nDoctor: You\'re welcome. Take care. And if you have any concerns in the meantime, don\'t hesitate to call our office. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\n[doctor] good afternoon alexis i understand you\'re having some issues with your hand\n[patient] yes i sit at the keyboard all day long and recently i\'ve been having some numbness and tingling in my right hand\n[doctor] okay how long has that been going on\n[patient] i do n\'t know probably on and off for six months but over the last month or so it\'s been a little more persistent\n[doctor] okay okay and when does that numbness and tingling do you get a lot of pain with it first let me ask that question\n[patient] some pain\n[doctor] some pain\n[patient] mostly when i\'m actually typing on the keyboard is when i experience the pain\n[doctor] okay\n[patient] but the numbness and tingling is all the time now\n[doctor] okay and when you get this pain what makes it better is it stopping and just resting for a while or do you take medications or try other\n[patient] i\'ve been taking ibuprofen so ibuprofen and seem like not typing on the keyboard helps the