[92mlitellm.acompletion(model='gpt-4o', api_base=None, messages=[{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 150/90 mmHg\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\nGrade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. Mild pain with flexion and extension but otherwise normal range of motion. There is swelling about the elbow, as well as mild ecchymosis.\n\nHematology/Lymphatic/Immunology\n- Examination: Bruising noted over the right elbow.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right elbow was reviewed and demonstrates no evidence of fracture or bony abnormalities.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Right elbow contusion.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient sustained an injury to his elbow when his son struck him with a lacrosse stick. His x-ray and exam findings are consistent with an elbow contusion.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to avoid playing lacrosse for the next couple of weeks, just until his elbow heals. After that, he should be fine to continue playing normally.\n- Medical Treatment: I\'m going to prescribe him ibuprofen 800 mg twice daily to help with some of the pain and swelling. He should also ice the area in 20-minute intervals 3 times per day to further reduce his symptoms.\n\n2. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: This appears to be well-controlled with dietary modifications and lisinopril.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged him to keep up with his dietary modifications.\n- Medical Treatment: We will keep him on lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: This appears to be well controlled at this time.\n- Medical Treatment: We will keep him on metformin 500 mg and order a hemoglobin A1c to evaluate any need for medication adjustments.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] Hello, how are you today?\n\n[patient] Hi, I\'m doing okay, but I\'ve been having some pain in my right elbow.\n\n[doctor] Hmm, I see. Can you tell me more about the pain in your right elbow?\n\n[patient] Sure, it started a few days ago. I was playing lacrosse with my son, and he accidentally hit me with the stick. Since then, my elbow has been really sore.\n\n[doctor] I understand. Can you describe the pain for me? Is it constant, or does it come and go?\n\n[patient] It\'s pretty constant, especially when I move my arm. It hurts more when I try to flex or extend it.\n\n[doctor] Got it. Have you noticed any swelling or bruising?\n\n[patient] Yes, there\'s some swelling and a bit of bruising around the area.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Have you tried any treatments or medications to relieve the pain?\n\n[patient] I\'ve been icing it, and I took some over-the-counter painkillers, but they don\'t seem to help much.\n\n[doctor] Alright. Let\'s go over your review of systems. Have you experienced any chest pain recently?\n\n[patient] No, no chest pain.\n\n[doctor] Any abdominal pain?\n\n[patient] No, my stomach\'s been fine.\n\n[doctor] Okay, how about any other joint pain besides your elbow?\n\n[patient] No, just the elbow.\n\n[doctor] Alright, let\'s check your vital signs. Your blood pressure is 150/90 mmHg. Have you been monitoring your blood pressure regularly?\n\n[patient] Yes, I have. I\'ve been trying to keep it under control with my diet.\n\n[doctor] Good to hear. Now, let\'s do a physical exam. I\'ll start with your lungs. Take a deep breath for me.\n\n[patient] (Takes a deep breath)\n\n[doctor] Your lungs sound clear bilaterally. Now, let\'s listen to your heart. (Listens with stethoscope) I hear a grade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur. Have you noticed any palpitations or unusual heartbeats?\n\n[patient] No, nothing like that.\n\n[doctor] Alright, let\'s move on to your elbow. I\'ll palpate the area. Let me know if you feel any pain.\n\n[patient] Ouch, yes, that hurts.\n\n[doctor] I can feel some tenderness over the olecranon area. How about when you flex and extend your elbow?\n\n[patient] It\'s painful, but I can still move it a bit.\n\n[doctor] There is some swelling and mild ecchymosis. I also noticed bruising over the area. We took an X-ray of your elbow, and it shows no evidence of fracture or bony abnormalities. \n\n[patient] That\'s good to hear.\n\n[doctor] Based on your symptoms and the X-ray, it looks like you have a right elbow contusion. This means you have a bruise on the bone, likely from the impact of the lacrosse stick.\n\n[patient] Okay, so what should I do about it?\n\n[doctor] I recommend avoiding playing lacrosse for the next couple of weeks to let your elbow heal. After that, you should be fine to continue playing normally. For now, I\'ll prescribe you ibuprofen 800 mg to take twice daily. This will help with the pain and swelling. Also, ice the area in 20-minute intervals three times a day.\n\n[patient] Alright, I can do that.\n\n[doctor] Great. Let\'s also discuss your other conditions. Your hypertension seems to be well-controlled with dietary modifications and lisinopril. Keep up with your dietary changes and continue taking lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n[patient] Will do.\n\n[doctor] Your diabetes also appears to be well-controlled. We\'ll keep you on metformin 500 mg, and I\'ll order a hemoglobin A1c test to evaluate if any adjustments are needed.\n\n[patient] Sounds good.\n\n[doctor] Do you have any questions or concerns about the treatment plan?\n\n[patient] No, I think that covers everything.\n\n[doctor] Excellent. If you have any issues or if the pain worsens, please don\'t hesitate to contact us. Otherwise, I\'ll see you in two weeks for a follow-up.\n\n[patient] Thank you, doctor.\n\n[doctor] You\'re welcome. Take care and get some rest.\n\n[patient] Will do. Thanks again.\n\n[doctor] Goodbye.\n\n[patient] Goodbye. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Good morning, how are you feeling today?\nPatient: Good morning, I\'m feeling okay.\nDoctor: I understand you have a chief complaint of right elbow pain. Can you tell me more about it?\nPatient: Yes, I got hit by my son with a lacrosse stick and now my right elbow hurts.\nDoctor: I see. Can you describe the pain for me?\nPatient: It hurts when I move my arm, especially when I bend it.\nDoctor: Okay, let\'s take a look at your vital signs. Your blood pressure is 150/90 mmHg, which is a bit high. Have you been experiencing any chest pain?\nPatient: No, I haven\'t.\nDoctor: Alright, let\'s move on to the physical exam. During the auscultation of your lungs, they were clear bilaterally. And I heard a grade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\nPatient: Hmm, okay.\nDoctor: When I examined your right elbow, there was pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. You also have mild pain with flexion and extension, but otherwise normal range of motion. There is swelling about the elbow, as well as mild ecchymosis.\nPatient: Yes, that\'s correct.\nDoctor: And I noticed some bruising over your right elbow.\nPatient: Yes, that\'s from the injury.\nDoctor: Alright, we did an x-ray of your right elbow and it showed no evidence of fracture or bony abnormalities.\nPatient: Okay.\nDoctor: Based on the examination and x-ray findings, I diagnose you with a right elbow contusion.\nPatient: What does that mean?\nDoctor: It means you have a bruised elbow, which is caused by the injury you sustained from your son hitting you with the lacrosse stick.\nPatient: Oh, okay.\nDoctor: To help with the pain and swelling, I\'m going to prescribe you ibuprofen 800 mg twice daily. You should also ice the area in 20-minute intervals 3 times per day.\nPatient: Alright, I\'ll do that.\nDoctor: And I encourage you to avoid playing lacrosse for the next couple of weeks, just until your elbow heals.\nPatient: Okay, I will.\nDoctor: I also see that you have hypertension, which is well-controlled with dietary modifications and lisinopril 20 mg daily. Please keep up with your dietary modifications.\nPatient: Okay, I will.\nDoctor: And your diabetes is well-controlled at this time, so we will keep you on metformin 500 mg and order a hemoglobin A1c to evaluate any need for medication adjustments.\nPatient: Alright, thank you.\nDoctor: You\'re welcome. Do you have any questions for me?\nPatient: No, I think you explained everything clearly.\nDoctor: Great, if you have any concerns or questions in the future, don\'t hesitate to come back and see me.\nPatient: Okay, thank you.\nDoctor: You\'re welcome. Take care.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\n[doctor] hey good to see you philip so i see here you\'re coming in you had some right elbow pain also you have a past medical history of hypertension diabetes we are gon na check up on those as well so can you tell me what happened to your elbow\n[patient] sure i hurt it about a week ago playing lacrosse with my kids just suddenly started hurting me when i was throwing the ball\n[doctor] okay okay so how old are your kids\n[patient] my oldest is thirteen and the other one is ten i was playing with a thirteen -year-old\n[doctor] okay yeah i know i i play with my kids sometimes and i think i\'m getting too old to do all that so i just i\'ve hurt myself before as well alright so with your your elbow what part of your elbow would you say hurts\n[patient] it\'s really like right at the point of the elbow right at right at like the tip of it\n[doctor] okay\n[patient] and it just it hurt hurts anytime i move it\n[doctor] anytime i move it do you remember falling\n[patient] no i do n\'t think so but my kid got pretty rough with me and was checking me several times with a sick\n[doctor] okay\n[patient] so i could have got hit there\n[doctor] okay alright yeah that that that definitely makes sense so how would you describe the pain is it like a dull pain is it stabbing what what would you say\n[patient] it it\'s a pretty sharp pain\n[doctor] okay\n[patient] anytime i move\n[doctor] okay and does it radiate down your arm or does it stay in that one spot\n[patient] it radiates down the forearm\n[doctor] radiates down the forearm alright so have you taken any medications for it to make it better\n[patient] i tried taking advil couple of days but i usually just forget about it\n[doctor] okay when you took it did it did it help at all did it change your your pain score\n[patient] yeah i i think it helped some\n[doctor] okay that\'s good alright so we\'ll we\'ll do a physical exam on that elbow we\'ll take a look at it in a second