r 40\'s, but I don\'t think anyone in my family has hypercholesterolemia.\n\n[doctor]: I see. That\'s important to note. Now, let\'s review your vitals. Your blood pressure looks good. Have you had any recent blood work done?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I had some tests done recently.\n\n[doctor]: Great. Let me take a look at those results. Your recent cholesterol level is elevated at 210. We\'ll need to do a full cholesterol panel, including LDL, HDL, and triglycerides, as well as a hemoglobin A1c, CMP, thyroid study, and a liver panel.\n\n[patient]: Okay, that sounds good.\n\n[doctor]: We\'ll also need to do some genetic testing to see if there\'s a hereditary component to your high cholesterol. \n\n[patient]: Alright, I understand.\n\n[doctor]: Let\'s go over your physical exam results. Your eyes are clear without any xanthomas, your neck is supple without thyromegaly, your lungs are clear bilaterally, and your heart has no murmurs, gallops, or rubs. Your abdomen also shows no hepatomegaly or splenomegaly.\n\n[patient]: That\'s good to know.\n\n[doctor]: Now, let\'s discuss the plan. The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends screening for cholesterol between the ages of 9 and 11, and again between 17 and 20. High cholesterol can increase the risk of pre-mature heart attacks.\n\n[patient]: That\'s interesting. I didn\'t know that.\n\n[doctor]: Yes, it\'s important to address this early. We\'ll also discuss a balanced diet with low saturated fats and sugars, and maintaining your physical activity. I\'ll refer you to a nutritionist who can help you with meal planning.\n\n[patient]: That sounds helpful. I could use some guidance on my diet.\n\n[doctor]: Great. We\'ll also schedule a follow-up in 3 to 6 months to check your cholesterol levels after you\'ve met with the nutritionist.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I can do that.\n\n[doctor]: Do you have any questions or concerns about the plan?\n\n[patient]: No, it all sounds good. I just want to make sure I\'m doing everything I can 