been icing her knees and wrapping her knees with an ACE bandage. She also takes ibuprofen or Aleve occasionally for pain. She states she tries to be as active as she can. She enjoys running outdoors. The patient adds that she would run 5 to 6 miles per day; however, she is unable to go 0.5 miles secondary to the pain. She denies any previous injuries to her knees.\n\nShe works from home.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of an upper extremity fracture when she was in 6th grade.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain and crepitus. Denies swelling or redness in the bilateral knees.\n\nVITALS\n\nAll vital signs are within the normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nGAIT: Normal, no deformity present.\nCV: Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs.\nNo edema\nNEURO: Patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical.\nMSK: Examination of the bilateral lower extremities: Abduction strength is 3/5. Remainder of muscle strength is 5/5. Examination of the bilateral knees: No overlying erythema, ecchymosis, or warmth of the skin appreciated. No effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. Knee pain with squatting. Negative Lachman test bilaterally. Negative anterior and posterior drawer tests bilaterally. Negative McMurray test bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the bilateral knees were reviewed today. These demonstrate no evidence of any fractures or osteoarthritis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nBilateral knee patellofemoral pain syndrome.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient regarding her current symptoms. I have explained to her that her symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome. We discussed treatment options and I have recommended that we begin with conservative treatment in the form of formal physical therapy to increase her lower extremity strength, mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics. I encouraged her to take running easy for now. She can continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatories as needed for pain. All questions were answered.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me soon.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Hi Sophia, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient]: Hi, I\'m doing okay, thanks.\n\n[doctor]: So, I see you\'re here for bilateral knee pain. Can you tell me more about what\'s been going on?\n\n[patient]: Sure. The pain started about six months ago, and I haven\'t had any specific injury. It\'s a deep, achy pain, and it\'s mostly on the back of my kneecaps.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, I see. Is the pain constant, or does it come and go?\n\n[patient]: It\'s pretty constant, but it gets worse when I move from sitting to standing or when I go up and down stairs.\n\n[doctor]: Got it. Have you noticed any swelling or redness in your knees?\n\n[patient]: No, there\'s no swelling or redness.\n\n[doctor]: Do you hear any strange noises from your knees, like creaking or popping?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I do hear a creaking sound sometimes.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, that\'s helpful to know. How about when you rest, does the pain get better?\n\n[patient]: Yes, it does get better when I rest.\n\n[doctor]: Have you tried anything to alleviate the pain?\n\n[patient]: I\'ve been icing my knees and wrapping them with an ACE bandage. I also take ibuprofen or Aleve occasionally.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s good. How often do you take those medications?\n\n[patient]: Maybe a couple of times a week when the pain gets really bad.\n\n[doctor]: Understood. You mentioned you enjoy running. How far do you usually run?\n\n[patient]: I usually run about 5 to 6 miles a day, but lately, I can\'t even go 0.5 miles because of the pain.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s quite a significant reduction. Have you had any previous injuries to your knees?\n\n[patient]: No, no previous injuries.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. Can you tell me about your medical history? Any other significant issues?\n\n[patient]: I had a fracture in my arm when I was in 6th grade, but that\'s about it.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. Do you have any other symptoms, like swelling or redness in your knees?\n\n[patient]: No, none of that.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, let\'s review your vital signs. They are all within normal limits. Now, I\'ll perform a physical exam. I\'ll start with your gait. Walk for me, please.\n\n[patient]: Sure. (Walks normally)\n\n[doctor]: Your gait looks normal, and I don\'t see any deformities. Let\'s check your heart. (Listens to heart) Your heart rate and rhythm are regular, no murmurs. Now, let\'s check your lower extremities. (Examines legs) No edema, and your patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical. Now, let\'s examine your knees. (Examines knees) There\'s no overlying erythema, ecchymosis, or warmth of the skin. No effusion. There is tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinaculum and a positive patellar grind test. You also have knee pain with squatting. The Lachman, anterior and posterior drawer, and McMurray tests are all negative bilaterally.\n\n[patient]: What does all that mean?\n\n[doctor]: Based on your symptoms and the physical exam, it seems like you have bilateral patellofemoral pain syndrome. This is a condition where the pain is around the front of the knee and the kneecap. It\'s common in people who are active and put a lot of stress on their knees.\n\n[patient]: Oh, I see. What can we do about it?\n\n[doctor]: We have a few options. First, I recommend starting with conservative treatment. I\'d like you to begin formal physical therapy to increase your lower extremity strength and mobility. It will also help correct any incorrect running mechanics that might be contributing to your pain.\n\n[patient]: That sounds good. What else?\n\n[doctor]: For now, you should take it easy on running. You can continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatories as needed for pain. We\'ll also monitor your progress and see if any further intervention is necessary.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I can do that.\n\n[doctor]: Great. We\'ll follow up soon to see how you\'re doing. Do you have any other questions?\n\n[patient]: No, I think that covers it. Thank you.\n\n[doctor]: You\'re welcome. Take care, and we\'ll see you soon. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ \nDoctor: Good morning, Sophia. I\'m Dr. Smith. How are you feeling today?\n\nPatient: Good morning, I\'m feeling okay.\n\nDoctor: Can you tell me what brings you in today?\n\nPatient: I have been having pain in both of my knees for the past 6 months.\n\nDoctor: I see. Can you describe the pain for me?\n\nPatient: It\'s a deep, achy pain. I feel it in the back of my knees.\n\nDoctor: Hmm, okay. And does anything make the pain worse or better?\n\nPatient: It gets worse when I stand up from a seated position or when I go up or down stairs. But it gets better when I rest.\n\nDoctor: I see. Have you tried anything to relieve the pain?\n\nPatient: Yes, I\'ve been icing my knees and wrapping them with an ACE bandage. I also take ibuprofen or Aleve sometimes.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you had any injuries to your knees in the past?\n\nPatient: No, I haven\'t had any injuries.\n\nDoctor: Alright. And what do you do for a living?\n\nPatient: I work from home.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And do you have any medical history that I should know about?\n\nPatient: I had an upper extremity fracture when I was in 6th grade.\n\nDoctor: Alright. Let\'s take a look at your knees now. Can you describe any swelling or redness in your knees?\n\nPatient: No, there\'s no swelling or redness.\n\nDoctor: Alright. Let\'s take your vital signs now. All of your vital signs are within normal limits.\n\nDoctor: Now, let\'s take a look at your knees. Your gait is normal and there\'s no deformity present. Your heart rate and rhythm are regular and there are no murmurs. There\'s no edema. Your patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical.\n\nDoctor: Let\'s take a look at your knees now. There\'s no overlying erythema, ecchymosis, or warmth of the skin. There\'s no effusion. But there is tenderness to palpation of the retinaculum. And the patellar grind test is positive. You experience knee pain when squatting. But the Lachman test, anterior and posterior drawer tests, and McMurray test are all negative.\n\nDoctor: We\'ve reviewed the x-rays of your knees and there\'s no evidence of any fractures or osteoarthritis.\n\nDoctor: Based on your examination and radiographic findings, I\'ve diagnosed you with bilateral knee patellofemoral pain syndrome. We\'ve discussed treatment options and I\'ve recommended that we start with conservative treatment in the form of physical therapy to increase your lower extremity strength, mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics. I encourage you to take it easy on running for now. You can continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatories as needed for pain.\n\nDoctor: Do you have any questions for me?\n\nPatient: No, I think you explained everything clearly.\n\nDoctor: Alright. You\'ll need to follow up with me soon.\n\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\n[doctor] hey sophia how are you doing today\n[patient] i\'ve been better my primary care doctor wanted me to see you because of this knee pain that i\'ve been having for about six months now\n[doctor] okay do you remember what caused the pain initially\n[patient] you know i really ca n\'t recall any specific event that caused the pain\n[doctor] alright well can can you describe the pain for me\n[patient] yeah so it\'s it\'s like a deep achy pain that it i feel like it\'s behind my kneecaps and sometimes i can even hear like what seems like like some creaking in my knees\n[doctor] hmmm alright now tell me what what type of activities makes that pain worse\n[patient] so i feel like the pain gets worse anytime i\'m like getting from like a seated position to standing so i i work from home so i\'m at my desk a lot so anytime i get up i have some