tape. She states that she is unable to take ibuprofen secondary to an ulcer.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the left knee: Edema and effusion noted. Moderate ROM. Pain with flexion and extension of the knee. Negative Lachman\'s. Negative valgus and varus stress test.\n\nRESULTS\n\n4 views of the left knee were taken. These reveal no evidence of any fractures or dislocations. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nLeft knee pain, possible MCL strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have discussed with the patient that her x-rays did not reveal any signs of a tear. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The plan is to place the patient in a knee immobilizer to provide increased support for the next week. I have also prescribed the patient tramadol 50 mg to treat her pain. She can take Tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain. I have also advised her to ice her left knee 3 to 4 times a day for 20 to 30 minutes at a time. I have advised her to refrain from running for the next 3 weeks. If her pain does not improve with the immobilizer, I will recommend obtaining an MRI.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Hi Angela, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient]: Hi, Doctor. I\'m doing okay, but I\'m in quite a bit of pain.\n\n[doctor]: I\'m sorry to hear that. I see you\'re here for left knee pain. Can you tell me more about what happened?\n\n[patient]: Yes, it started about a week ago. I was running around with my niece and I fell. I heard a pop in my knee and it\'s been hurting ever since.\n\n[doctor]: I see. When you say it\'s been hurting, can you describe the pain for me? Is it sharp, dull, throbbing?\n\n[patient]: It\'s more of a throbbing pain. It\'s mostly on the inside and outside of my knee.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, okay. On a scale from 1 to 10, how would you rate your pain?\n\n[patient]: I\'d say it\'s about a 4 right now, but it\'s getting worse.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. Have you noticed any swelling or any other symptoms?\n\n[patient]: Yes, there\'s definitely swelling. I\'ve also been limping because of the pain.\n\n[doctor]: Have you tried any treatments or medications to help with the pain?\n\n[patient]: I\'ve been taking Tylenol. It helps a little, but not much. I\'ve also been wrapping my knee with kinesiology tape.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, that\'s good to know. Are you able to take any other pain medications, like ibuprofen?\n\n[patient]: No, I can\'t take ibuprofen because I have an ulcer.\n\n[doctor]: Got it. Thanks for letting me know. Have you had any issues with your knee before this injury?\n\n[patient]: No, this is the first time.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, let\'s move on to the physical exam. I\'m going to take a look at your knee now. Can you point to where it hurts the most?\n\n[patient]: It\'s right here on the inside and outside of my knee.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, I\'m going to press gently around that area. Let me know if it hurts.\n\n[patient]: Ouch, yes, that hurts.\n\n[doctor]: I see there\'s some swelling and effusion. I\'m also going to check your range of motion. Can you bend and straighten your knee for me?\n\n[patient]: (Bends and straightens knee) Ouch, that hurts too.\n\n[doctor]: I can see that there\'s pain with both flexion and extension. Let\'s check a few more things. I\'m going to do a Lachman\'s test and a valgus and varus stress test. These might be a bit uncomfortable.\n\n[patient]: Okay.\n\n[doctor]: (Performs tests) The Lachman\'s test is negative, and both the valgus and varus stress tests are negative. That\'s good news. Now, I\'m going to order some x-rays to rule out any fractures or dislocations. We\'ll take four views of your knee.\n\n[patient]: Alright.\n\n[doctor]: We\'ll wait for the results of the x-rays. In the meantime, I have some recommendations for you. First, I want you to wear a knee immobilizer for the next week to provide support.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I can do that.\n\n[doctor]: I\'m also going to prescribe you tramadol 50 mg for the pain. You can take it as needed, and continue using Tylenol for breakthrough pain.\n\n[patient]: Thank you. Will I need to do anything else?\n\n[doctor]: Yes, I want you to ice your knee 3 to 4 times a day for 20 to 30 minutes at a time. This will help reduce the swelling.\n\n[patient]: Alright, I can do that.\n\n[doctor]: Also, try to avoid running for the next 3 weeks. If your pain doesn\'t improve with the immobilizer, we may need to get an MRI.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I understand.\n\n[doctor]: Great. Do you have any questions or concerns?\n\n[patient]: No, I think that covers everything.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, we\'ll review the x-ray results once they come in. If there\'s anything else we need to address, we\'ll discuss it then.\n\n[patient]: Thank you, Doctor.\n\n[doctor]: You\'re welcome. Take care, Angela. We\'ll see you soon.\n\n[patient]: Thanks, you too. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Good afternoon, Angela. How are you feeling today?\nPatient: Hmm, I\'m okay, thank you.\nDoctor: So, you presented to the clinic today for the evaluation of left knee pain. Can you tell me more about the onset of your pain?\nPatient: Yes, it started about a week ago when I was running with my niece and I fell. I heard a pop and I think I twisted my knee.\nDoctor: I see. And since the injury, you\'ve been limping and experiencing a throbbing pain in the medial and lateral aspects of your knee, is that correct?\nPatient: Yes, that\'s right.\nDoctor: And how would you rate your pain level on a scale of 1 to 10?\nPatient: I would say it\'s about a 4, but it\'s increasing.\nDoctor: Okay, I understand. Have you tried any pain relief medication?\nPatient: Yes, I\'ve been taking Tylenol and it\'s provided mild relief, but I\'ve also been wrapping my knee with kinesiology tape.\nDoctor: I see. And have you been able to take ibuprofen?\nPatient: No, I can\'t take ibuprofen because it would irritate an ulcer I