 to prescribe you tramadol 50 mg for the pain. You can take it as needed, and continue using Tylenol for breakthrough pain.\n\n[patient]: Thank you. Will I need to do anything else?\n\n[doctor]: Yes, I want you to ice your knee 3 to 4 times a day for 20 to 30 minutes at a time. This will help reduce the swelling.\n\n[patient]: Alright, I can do that.\n\n[doctor]: Also, try to avoid running for the next 3 weeks. If your pain doesn\'t improve with the immobilizer, we may need to get an MRI.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I understand.\n\n[doctor]: Great. Do you have any questions or concerns?\n\n[patient]: No, I think that covers everything.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, we\'ll review the x-ray results once they come in. If there\'s anything else we need to address, we\'ll discuss it then.\n\n[patient]: Thank you, Doctor.\n\n[doctor]: You\'re welcome. Take care, Angela. We\'ll see you soon.\n\n[patient]: Thanks, you too. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Good afternoon, Angela. How are you feeling today?\nPatient: Hmm, I\'m okay, thank you.\nDoctor: So, you presented to the clinic today for the evaluation of left knee pain. Can you tell me more about the onset of your pain?\nPatient: Yes, it started about a week ago when I was running with my niece and I fell. I heard a pop and I think I twisted my knee.\nDoctor: I see. And since the injury, you\'ve been limping and experiencing a throbbing pain in the medial and lateral aspects of your knee, is that correct?\nPatient: Yes, that\'s right.\nDoctor: And how would you rate your pain level on a scale of 1 to 10?\nPatient: I would say it\'s about a 4, but it\'s increasing.\nDoctor: Okay, I understand. Have you tried any pain relief medication?\nPatient: Yes, I\'ve been taking Tylenol and it\'s provided mild relief, but I\'ve also been wrapping my knee with kinesiology tape.\nDoctor: I see. And have you been able to take ibuprofen?\nPatient: No, I can\'t take ibuprofen because it would irritate an ulcer I have.\nDoctor: Alright, I understand. During your physical exam, we found edema and effusion in your left knee. You also had moderate ROM and pain with flexion and extension of the knee. The Lachman\'s test was negative, as well as the valgus and varus stress test. Do you have any questions about these findings?\nPatient: No, not really.\nDoctor: Okay. We took 4 views of your left knee and found no evidence of any fractures or dislocations. No other abnormalities were noted. Based on your examination and radiographic findings, it appears that you have a possible MCL strain.\nPatient: Okay.\nDoctor: We\'re going to treat your knee conservatively. We\'ll put you in a knee immobilizer to provide increased support for the next week and prescribe tramadol 50 mg to treat your pain. You can also take Tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain. I advise you to ice your left knee 3 to 4 times a day for 20 to 30 minutes at a time and refrain from running for the next 3 weeks. If your pain does not improve with the immobilizer, we\'ll need to obtain an MRI.\nPatient: Okay, I understand.\nDoctor: Great. If you have any questions or concerns, please don\'t hesitate to contact me. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\n[doctor] angela good to see you today so i\'m writing here in my notes that you\'re you\'re coming in you had some left knee pain so how did you hurt your knee\n[patient] yeah so i hurt my knee the other day when i was running around with my niece she she is learning how to take off the training wheels for her bike\n[doctor] mm-hmm\n[patient] and i fell and i think i twisted my knee in some kind of funky way\n[doctor] okay that that that that\'s not good how old are your niece\n[patient] she is five and perfect\n[doctor] well that\'s good that you\'re trying to help her with the learning how to ride a bike\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] that sounds fun but i\'m i\'m sad as you hurt your knee doing it so for your knee pain are you are you able to bear weight on that leg the left leg\n[patient] well i\'